window.shHubContent.receive("notes",{"readings":{"intro":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>The definition of intellectual disability and its three DSM-5 parts, including onset in the developmental period</li><li>How the terms changed: feeble-minded, mental retardation, Rosa's Law (2010), DSM-5 (2013)</li><li>Person-first language: a diagnosis, not a description</li><li>Institutions, deinstitutionalization, group homes and day programs</li><li>Prevalence of disability, Alabama's numbers, and why the counts are rising</li><li>Access to care: Medicaid until 21, the Surgeon General's reports, CODA 2019 and the ADA Code of Ethics 2018</li><li>Etiology, including genetic causes and adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)</li><li>General characteristics and how to help people with ID learn</li></ul></div>\n<p>Dr. Stephen Mitchell directs the Sparks Dental Clinic at UAB, where you will rotate over the next couple of years. This first lecture asks why dental school needs a class on special needs dentistry at all: what the population looks like, how the words for it have changed, how its care has changed, how common it is, where it comes from, and what its members share.</p>\n<h4>Who counts as special needs</h4>\n<p>Special needs can look very different. An autistic boy may look like any other child until a sound upsets him and he covers his ears. A person with Down syndrome usually has facial features you recognize at once. A person with cerebral palsy may use a wheelchair, and CP ranges from only the legs being affected to someone who cannot walk. And a large share of the special needs patients you will see have <b>no defined diagnosis or syndrome</b> at all.</p>\n<p>Special needs is a broad group: medically compromised patients, people who have been injured, people with mental illness, and a very large number of elderly patients. Most of this course is about one subset: people with <b>intellectual disability (ID)</b>, also called <b>intellectual and developmental disability (IDD)</b>. The current terms stress that this is a disability and a medical diagnosis, not a type of person.</p>\n<h4>The definition</h4>\n<p>Intellectual disability is a set of <b>deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning that begin during the developmental period</b>. Intellectual means the way the mind processes information is different and makes intellectual tasks hard. <b>Adaptive</b> functioning is the ability to adapt to a setting and to new sensory input.</p>\n<p>The <b>DSM-5</b> (the American Psychiatric Association's Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, fifth edition) breaks the diagnosis into three parts, and all three must be present:</p>\n<ul><li>Deficits in intellectual functioning</li><li>Deficits in adaptive functioning</li><li><b>Onset during the developmental period</b> (childhood)</li></ul>\n<p>So a person who develops normally and then has a brain injury as a young adult is special needs, and may have lost intellectual and adaptive function, but does not technically have ID, because it did not start in childhood. Dr. Mitchell's example: a patient his own age who was shot in the head in his senior year of high school. He lost his short-term memory and his ability to adapt to the dental setting, but his diagnosis is a traumatic injury, not ID.</p>\n<h4>Beyond the IQ test</h4>\n<p>Older testing relied on an <b>IQ test</b>: a score <b>under 70</b> qualified a person as intellectually disabled. Today's assessment is <b>comprehensive</b>: clinical assessment over a long time by several specialists (at the Civitan-Sparks Center, often half a day or a whole day), not one test. The DSM-5 adds adaptive functioning, which matters most in <b>autism</b>: some autistic people are very bright but cannot adapt to odd sounds, tastes or smells. Modern testing also weighs <b>cultural and linguistic factors</b>: a person who cannot speak well may, given a new communication tool, turn out to understand far more than anyone thought.</p>\n<h4>How the words changed</h4>\n<p>The medical community introduces a term; the culture picks it up and uses it as an insult; the term becomes a stigma and is retired; a new one takes its place. In the early 1900s the category was <b>feeble-minded</b>, divided by mental age into <b>moron</b> (7-12 years), <b>imbecile</b> (2-7) and <b>idiot</b> (under 2). Those were textbook medical terms. Then came <b>mental retardation</b>, graded mild, moderate, severe and profound by IQ. In <b>2010</b>, <b>Rosa's Law</b> removed \"mental retardation\" from U.S. federal documents in favor of intellectual disability, and in <b>2013 the DSM-5</b> made the same change.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Era</th><th>Term</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Early 1900s</td><td>Feeble-minded: moron (mental age 7-12), imbecile (2-7), idiot (under 2)</td><td>Medical terms at the time; culture turned them into insults</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mid 1900s to 2010</td><td>Mental retardation: mild, moderate, severe, profound, by IQ (under 70)</td><td>Became a playground insult</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2010</td><td>Rosa's Law</td><td>Removed \"mental retardation\" from U.S. federal documents</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2013</td><td>DSM-5</td><td>Made the same change: intellectual disability</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Today</td><td>Intellectual disability (ID) or intellectual and developmental disability (IDD)</td><td>A diagnosis, not a description: person first</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Mental age is still useful, just not as a label. Dr. Mitchell's Sparks medical history asks what age the patient seems to understand at, because \"acts about like a 10-year-old\" and \"understands like an 8-month-old\" call for very different communication.</p>\n<h4>Person first</h4>\n<p><b>Intellectual disability is a diagnostic condition, not a description.</b> Put the person ahead of the disorder: \"a patient who has Down syndrome,\" not \"a Down syndrome patient.\" Many people with ID, and their families, do not want to be defined by the disability. In Dr. Mitchell's experience, the more respectfully you speak to these patients, often at a higher level than they are used to, the more they relax and do their best for you.</p>\n<p>Other special needs groups you will treat: medically compromised patients (cancer treatment, diabetes), patients disabled by injury (a man at Sparks paralyzed from the neck down in a car accident 25-30 years ago), and patients with mental illness (from PTSD and anxiety attacks to depression).</p>\n<h4>Where care happened: institutions</h4>\n<p>Before the 1950s most people with moderate and profound ID lived in large <b>institutions</b>, and doctors urged families to send them there. Bryce Hospital in Tuscaloosa was the largest in Alabama. At first institutions may have offered round-the-clock therapy, but funding and staff were cut while the number of residents grew, and they became places where people were locked out of sight and mistreated. In the 1970s, <b>Geraldo Rivera</b>'s undercover report from <b>Willowbrook</b> in New York exposed the conditions and helped change how the nation treated people with ID. (The documentary <i>Unforgotten: 25 Years After Willowbrook</i> is optional.)</p>\n<h4>Deinstitutionalization and community care</h4>\n<p>The 1970s brought a big push for <b>deinstitutionalization</b>: caring for people with ID in the community where they live. It is still the main model today. By <b>1978</b> national laws required <b>public schools</b> to provide special education. During COVID, children with special needs lost the therapy they get at school, which is why families pushed to reopen schools.</p>\n<p>Adults go to <b>day programs</b> during regular school hours: socialization and therapy, then home. Local examples are <b>United Ability</b> (formerly United Cerebral Palsy), the <b>Exceptional Foundation</b> in Homewood, and the <b>Arc of Central Alabama</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Most</b> people with ID live at home with family. Many adults live in <b>group homes</b>: a house with about <b>6-8 clients</b> at similar levels and a caregiver, who help with brushing, bathing, dressing, laundry and getting to appointments (Arc PHP, the Learning Tree). The model is good in principle but underfunded, the work is hard and poorly paid, and staff burn out. <b>Institutional living</b> still exists for only <b>1-2%</b>, the most profoundly affected or those at risk of harming themselves or others; <b>Glenwood</b> is the Birmingham example.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Who</th><th>Examples from the lecture</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Home with family</td><td>Most people with ID</td><td>Many go to a day program during the day</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Day programs</td><td>Adults who live at home or in a group home</td><td>United Ability (formerly United Cerebral Palsy), the Exceptional Foundation, the Arc of Central Alabama</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Group homes</td><td>About 6-8 clients at a similar level, with caregivers</td><td>Arc PHP, the Learning Tree; they bring clients to appointments</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Institutions</td><td>Only 1-2%, the most profoundly affected</td><td>Glenwood (Birmingham)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>How common is it?</h4>\n<p>By the U.S. government's broad definition, <b>1 in 5 adults</b> (over 53 million) has some disability, from about 1 in 6 in Minnesota to <b>1 in 3 in Alabama, which leads the nation</b>. The most common is <b>mobility</b>, much of it from aging. General disability is a little more common in <b>females</b>, and highest in African Americans, then Hispanics, then whites. A definition that broad makes Alabama hesitant to fund disability services.</p>\n<p><b>Severe disability</b> is closer to the population Sparks treats: <b>12.6%</b> nationally (2015), and <b>16.7%</b> in Alabama, about 800,000 people, projected to reach 814,000 by 2030. Mississippi is 16.2%, Arkansas 17.1% and Kentucky 17%; <b>West Virginia leads the country at 19.4%</b>. For severe disability, males and females are the same (12.5 vs 12.7%), whites and African Americans are the same, Native Americans are highest, and Hispanics are low.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Any disability</th><th>Severe disability</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>U.S.</td><td>1 in 5 adults (over 53 million)</td><td>12.6% (about 39 million, 2015)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alabama</td><td>1 in 3 adults: leads the nation</td><td>16.7% (about 800,000; 814,000 projected by 2030)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Most common type</td><td>Mobility (much of it the elderly)</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sex</td><td>Females a little higher</td><td>Male and female the same (12.5 vs 12.7%)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Race</td><td>African Americans, then Hispanics, then whites</td><td>Whites and African Americans the same; Native Americans highest; Hispanics low</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Highest state</td><td></td><td>West Virginia 19.4%; Arkansas 17.1%, Kentucky 17%</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <b>National Health Interview Survey</b> found developmental disabilities in children rose <b>9.5%</b> from 2009-2017 over the previous period and <b>38%</b> since 1997; about <b>1 in 6 children (18%)</b> now has one. Part of the rise is good news: less stigma (families no longer hide a child), better diagnosis, and wider categories. Autism and ADHD have risen sharply, largely from diagnosis (many older Sparks patients carry an ID or even \"mental retardation\" diagnosis who would be on the autism spectrum today). But some definable disabilities, <b>cerebral palsy, hearing loss and seizure disorders, have declined</b>.</p>\n<h4>Unmet dental need</h4>\n<p>The <b>U.S. Surgeon General</b> reported in <b>2000</b> that people with disabilities have a significant access-to-care problem, in <b>2002</b> that oral health care for them showed a large disparity and a lack of providers, and in <b>2005</b> tied the problem to training: dental and medical schools were not teaching enough, so graduates felt unequipped. Offices simply turned families away. About <b>29%</b> of people with disabilities could not get dental care because of cost, and those with Medicaid often could not find a dentist who took it.</p>\n<p><b>Alabama Medicaid</b> covers most people with ID <b>until their 21st birthday</b>, and covers them well, including care in the operating room at Children's. Blue Cross Blue Shield and ALL Kids also allow OR access; most other dental plans make it nearly impossible. After 21, people with severe ID usually keep Medicaid <b>medical</b> coverage, but the <b>dental benefit ends at 21</b>. The Affordable Care Act may keep someone on a parent's private insurance to about 26; after that most have no dental insurance and pay out of pocket, often from a Social Security check. Some higher-functioning patients work (bagging groceries, United Ability's document-shredding workshop), but not enough to pay for major care.</p>\n<h4>Etiology</h4>\n<p>In <b>30-40%</b> of cases no cause can be found (Dr. Mitchell thinks more). The more profound the disability, the more likely a cause is found. Causes are biological, psychosocial, or both.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Genetic</b>: by 2013, 280 genes were linked to ID (the number itself doesn't matter: more are found all the time), and some studies put up to 55% of cases as genetic. Two kinds: <b>chromosomal aberrations</b> (Down syndrome, three copies of chromosome 21) and <b>single-gene</b> errors (fragile X). Errors on the <b>sex chromosomes</b> show more often, because there is no second healthy copy to mask them.</li><li><b>Physical, prenatal</b>: maternal malnutrition, acute or chronic infection, and <b>fetal alcohol syndrome</b>, about <b>2%</b> of ID.</li><li><b>Physical, neonatal</b>: prematurity, a hypoxic event at birth, birth injury (some cerebral palsy).</li><li><b>Physical, postnatal</b>: injury, tumors, infections, toxins.</li><li><b>Environmental and psychosocial</b>: lack of stimulation early in life.</li></ul>\n<h4>Adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)</h4>\n<p>Children who face many negative experiences while the brain develops wire it differently. <b>Toxic stress</b> releases too much <b>cortisol and adrenaline</b>, overstimulating some brain areas and understimulating others, and the effect is <b>dose-related</b>: the more ACEs, the bigger the measurable impact, from risky behavior and disease to early death. People with many ACEs are about <b>15 times more likely to attempt suicide</b>, and more likely to smoke, drink, miss work, and have job and money problems. With four or more ACEs, about 1 in 4 has serious financial trouble, against 1 in 10 with none; so most still do not, but they are more susceptible.</p>\n<p>The biology: hormones change cells, <b>cells die in the hippocampus</b> (memories are stored and recalled less accurately), circuits change, and the stress state stays raised, which drives <b>fear-based responses</b>. The child finds it harder to tell safe from dangerous and reads more situations as threats. That is a patient who is on edge in your chair.</p>\n<h4>General characteristics</h4>\n<p>No physical feature covers everyone with special needs, though syndromes have their own features. You may see more unsteady gait and poor coordination, scoliosis, orofacial differences and flaccid muscles. Most of Dr. Mitchell's patients with ID have some <b>speech problem</b>; many show <b>repetitive actions</b>, <b>hypersensitivity to stimuli</b> (sounds, tastes, a sensitive <b>gag reflex</b>) and <b>emotional swings</b>, with the coping skills of a child.</p>\n<p>Behavior is what scares providers most, but a large share of these patients behave wonderfully, and many are turned away for what might happen rather than what does. Intellect varies widely too: some autistic people are above average, some people with ID work at Publix, and some with Down syndrome or autism act on television.</p>\n<h4>How people with ID learn best</h4>\n<ul><li>They often <b>process sounds more slowly</b>: explain clearly, in short terms</li><li><b>Short-term memory</b> is often weak: repeat often</li><li>Use <b>concrete, specific language</b>; abstract cues may not be understood</li><li>Avoid <b>overstimulation</b> (too much noise, taste or water): keep things quiet</li><li><b>Routine</b> is very important to their emotional stability</li></ul>\n<h4>Why it's your job</h4>\n<p>This population is the minority group facing the greatest challenge in getting dental care, and real discrimination. Before 2019, <b>CODA</b> Standard 2 required graduates to be competent in <b>assessing</b> the treatment needs of patients with special needs; in <b>2019</b> it became <b>\"assessing and managing\"</b>. In <b>2018</b> the <b>ADA Code of Ethics</b> added <b>disability</b> to the reasons (with race and national origin) it is unethical to refuse a patient.</p>\n<p>That does not mean every dentist must treat every patient with a disability. It means you cannot just say \"we can't help you\" and hang up. If the care is beyond your skill, you are ethically responsible for <b>connecting the family with a dentist who can treat them</b>: refer. With so few places that will, the hope is that some of you will decide you can see a few of these patients yourselves.</p>\n","behavior":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Definitions: maladaptive behavior, non-contingent escape, protective stabilization, implied and express consent</li><li>The two limits on care: ability to benefit and ability to tolerate</li><li>Communication tools: social age, starting high, visual cues, positive reinforcement, clear and limited goals</li><li>Environmental tools: positioning, water in short bursts, lights, music, fewer people, routine</li><li>Active vs passive stabilization, with examples; why it's used and when it never should be</li><li>Characteristics of ideal stabilization; Molt prop placement; the papoose board</li><li>Informed consent: three elements, documentation, and the ethics (competence, beneficence, advocacy)</li></ul></div>\n<p>Concern about behavior is one of the main reasons dentists hesitate to treat people with intellectual and developmental disabilities. Dr. Mitchell's second lecture covers general behavior tools you can use in any practice, then more advanced techniques (cumbersome and legally riskier) that you will see at Sparks but may not use in your own office. The readings are from the Special Care Advocates in Dentistry website: <b>Module 2</b> (behavioral supports and special communication, up to sedation, which is beyond this course) and <b>Module 6</b> (managing maladaptive behaviors, restraints and positioning devices).</p>\n<h4>Maladaptive behavior</h4>\n<p><b>Maladaptive behavior</b> is the academic term for a patient who <b>cannot adapt to the treatment conditions</b> in a dental office (\"throwing a fit,\" \"cutting up\"). Helping a patient adapt often improves behavior. It applies to only a <b>subset</b> of the IDD population: many adapt very well. The trouble is that it is <b>unpredictable</b>.</p>\n<h4>Quality care, limited only by two things</h4>\n<p>Patients with IDD deserve quality care limited only by:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Their ability to benefit from the treatment.</b> Does a profoundly disabled patient benefit from esthetic anterior crowns over stained but healthy enamel? Does a CP patient with a severe gag reflex benefit from a denture or partial they can't wear?</li><li><b>Their ability to tolerate the treatment.</b> A patient might benefit from tooth-colored restorations (or an implant) but cannot keep the gingiva healthy around them. A tooth might be savable with a large restoration or cast crown, but maladaptive behavior makes that dangerous, and the tooth must come out.</li></ul>\n<p>The behavioral supports come in three groups: <b>communication</b>, <b>environment</b> and <b>protective stabilization</b>.</p>\n<h4>Communication: the most used, least invasive tool</h4>\n<p>Mental processing is slower in patients with IDD and short-term memory may be weak, so:</p>\n<ul><li>Allow more time for pre-op, operative and post-op instructions</li><li>Keep instructions <b>short and clear</b>; avoid abstract terms and speak concretely</li><li><b>Verbal reinforcement</b>: repeat instructions often</li><li>Reinforce home care at every visit</li><li>Even a patient who understands no words understands <b>tone and emotion</b>: anxiety, anger or irritation ramps a situation up; calm and compassion bring a response in kind</li><li>Include the <b>caregivers</b></li></ul>\n<p>Speak at the patient's <b>social age</b>: the level of skill the patient has acquired. A useful medical history question: <i>at what level does the patient understand?</i> Normal for age, no understanding, or limited understanding \"like a ___-year-old.\" Also ask what the patient can do alone.</p>\n<p>Dr. Mitchell asked for special attention to this: <b>always start a conversation at a normal level, then back down</b> to the social level you find. Many patients understand far more than we give them credit for, and talking down to them is offensive and shuts them down. If you must err, talk too high and back down.</p>\n<p><b>Visual cues</b>: say \"I need you to open your mouth,\" then pull your mask down and open yours. Let the patient see and touch the prophy angle.</p>\n<p><b>Positive reinforcement</b>: behavior is shaped better by rewarding the positive than by correcting the negative (\"more flies with honey\"). In patients with IDD, emotion and feelings often drive behavior, so keep feelings positive: praise desired behavior heavily; correct negative behavior briefly (\"no, we can't do that\"), then praise as soon as they follow.</p>\n<p><b>Clear and limited goals</b>: tell the patient what you are going to do, what they need to do, and how long it will take. \"I'm going to lay the chair down like a bed. I need you to lay back against the chair. It will take until the count of 10 to stop.\"</p>\n<h4>Non-contingent escape</h4>\n<p>In <b>non-contingent escape</b>, the doctor sets a specific, small treatment goal and <b>does not stop until it is reached</b>, even if behavior worsens. Stopping because of the behavior would be <b>contingent escape</b>: the patient learns that acting out ends treatment. Two ways to set the goal:</p>\n<ul><li><b>A time</b>: \"We are going to wash your tooth for five seconds,\" then count down. Simple repeated direction, a visual cue with the hand, counting, and setting the expectation for next time.</li><li><b>A number of teeth</b>: \"We are going to clean three teeth,\" counting teeth as you go. The patient resists at two, and you finish the third before the break.</li></ul>\n<p>Keep the intervals <b>consistent</b> so the patient settles into a rhythm; as they relax, you may lengthen them.</p>\n<h4>The environment</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Positioning</b>: some patients won't lie back; seat them at about <b>45 degrees</b>. Some won't sit in the dental chair at all; Sparks has done cleanings in the caregivers' chairs.</li><li><b>Gag reflex</b>: a continuous air-water spray floods a patient who can't swallow well, and they fight because they can't breathe. Spray in <b>short bursts</b>, in and out.</li><li><b>Music</b>: some patients love a particular artist.</li><li><b>Light</b>: a light-sensitive patient opened right up once the overhead lights were off and only the unit light was used.</li><li><b>Hyperdistractibility</b>: fewer people in the operatory, or an isolated operatory.</li><li><b>Routine</b>: the same operatory and the same staff each time.</li></ul>\n<h4>Physical restraint and protective stabilization</h4>\n<p>The <b>Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services</b> define a physical restraint broadly: (A) any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material or equipment that immobilizes or reduces a patient's ability to move arms, legs, body or head freely; (B) a drug used to restrict behavior or movement that is not standard treatment or dosage for the patient's condition (sedation: beyond this course).</p>\n<p>The <b>AAPD</b> term, and the one Dr. Mitchell uses, is <b>protective stabilization</b>: \"the physical limitation of a patient's movement <b>by a person or restrictive equipment</b>, materials or devices <b>for a finite period of time</b> in order to safely provide examination, diagnosis and/or treatment.\" A person holding a patient still <i>is</i> protective stabilization.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Active</b> stabilization: a <b>person</b> holds the patient still (Module 6 calls it \"physical restraint\").</li><li><b>Passive</b> stabilization: a <b>device</b> holds the patient still (Module 6: \"mechanical restraint\").</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Active</th><th>Passive</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>What holds the patient</td><td>A person</td><td>A device</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Module 6 calls it</td><td>Physical restraint</td><td>Mechanical restraint</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Head holds, hand guarding, therapeutic holds of arms and legs</td><td>Mouth props (Molt, Open-Wide), papoose board / Rainbow Wrap</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Needs informed consent?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Why use it?</b> It allows treatment, provides <b>safety for the patient and staff</b>, and makes care more efficient. Think of an infant car seat: it restrains, the infant may fuss, and not using one would be negligent. Stabilization is predominantly about safety.</p>\n<p><b>Never use it as</b> punishment (\"if you don't straighten up, you're going in the papoose\"), a substitute for guiding behavior, or a convenience for the practitioner (clinics that put every child in a papoose board to move faster caused real problems for the profession).</p>\n<p><b>Good protective stabilization</b> has the <b>lowest potential to cause injury</b>, is the <b>least restrictive alternative</b> (if only the head needs holding, hold only the head), and is used <b>only with informed consent</b>. Its use should be short, limit movement, prevent injury, let staff finish the needed treatment, and (hopefully) be tolerated. Some patients with IDD actually calm down in it.</p>\n<p>Module 6 puts it strongly: where indicated, for physically resistive patients, restraints should be used without reservation, and treating a very resistive patient without them may even be considered <b>negligence</b>. Dr. Mitchell learned it the hard way when a patient hit one of his staff.</p>\n<h4>Active stabilization techniques</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Head hold to the operator's side</b>: cradle the head between the dentist's \"mirror arm\" and chest, like a football. You can move with the patient and anticipate movement.</li><li><b>Staff cradle the head with hands</b> (more resistant patients): a staff member kneels or sits at the head of the chair, fingers spread, resting on the <b>temporal and occipital bones</b>, spreading force over the whole hand so no spot bruises. Relax as the patient relaxes, but stay ready.</li><li><b>Module 6's method</b> uses the forearms on the head (fewer finger marks). Dr. Mitchell finds it uncomfortable for the shoulders and unstable: it doesn't stop the patient lifting the head.</li><li><b>Hand guarding</b>: hands laid gently over the patient's hands. It reminds them where their hands go and guards against sudden movement; more a passive touch than a restraint, though it can become one.</li><li><b>Therapeutic holds</b> prevent violent behavior. Arms (scratching, hitting): hold securely at the wrists, cross the arms at hip or waist level, hold the hands to the sides. Legs (kicking, pushing): a strap across the knees so they can't buckle, and cross and hold the ankles. Dr. Mitchell's own technique: cup under the ankles with open hands and <b>lift the legs off the chair</b> (higher if the patient arches), so they can't dig in their heels; the legs may move, but the head stays still.</li></ul>\n<p>With any hold, <b>check circulation</b>, and ease off as the patient relaxes: easing off becomes positive reinforcement. Module 6 also describes <b>HOME</b> (hand-over-mouth exercise) and <b>HOMAR</b> (hand-over-mouth, airway restricted): <b>do not use them</b>. HOMAR is not used at all anymore, and HOME only rarely, with extensive training.</p>\n<h4>Passive stabilization: mouth props</h4>\n<p>Most often a mouth prop is used <b>with the patient's consent</b>, to help them stay open without tiring. As a stabilizer it can force an unwilling mouth open so treatment is safe. It protects the patient from biting on a sharp or hard object (a handpiece or bur) and cutting tissue or breaking a tooth, and protects the provider from being bitten. Without a prop, keep your fingers in the <b>buccal vestibule</b>, never across the teeth.</p>\n<p>The <b>Molt mouth prop</b> is the most common in pediatric and special needs dentistry. It opens like scissors, latches at any opening, and only closes with its release lever. Hold it from outside the mouth. Keep the blades on the <b>posterior teeth</b>: slipped forward onto anterior teeth they can <b>luxate or even avulse</b> them, and lips can get caught under a blade and lacerated. The cheek side can cause minor abrasion or pinching: <b>report any trauma to the caregiver</b>. Sparks uses bare blades because the silicone covers can come off and become a choking hazard.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Where you'll see it</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Molt mouth prop</td><td>Pediatric and special needs dentistry</td><td>Opens like scissors and latches at any opening; hold it from outside; blades on the posterior teeth; no silicone covers (choking hazard)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>McKesson bite block</td><td>The second-floor and other adult clinics</td><td>Rubber wedge; not used in special needs or pediatric dentistry; patients can crush it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Open-Wide mouth prop</td><td>At home (Specialized Care Co.)</td><td>Wood core in dense foam; about $20 for four on Amazon</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Passive stabilization: the papoose board</h4>\n<p><b>Papoose Board</b> is a brand: an inflexible padded board, wrist straps and soft canvas body wraps that close with Velcro. It has become the \"Kleenex\" word for any such device. Sparks uses the <b>Rainbow Wrap</b> (Specialized Care Company), which has a hinge so the patient can sit up; they call it \"the blanket,\" and since COVID they lay a sheet between the patient and the wrap. Placing a patient: secure each wrist at the wrist bone, snugly (roll a larger patient a little); the leg wrap goes over the knees so they can't buckle; lay the chair back; pull the wraps tight, ideally with two people, crossing them in an <b>X over the chest</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Weighted blankets</b> are popular in autism: they don't restrain, they comfort with deep sensory input, a bit like swaddling. The problem in dentistry is disinfecting them between patients.</p>\n<h4>Legal and ethical considerations</h4>\n<p><b>Parental acceptance</b>: studies of large parent populations show more and more parents find restraints unacceptable. In Dr. Mitchell's experience (not studies), families of patients with moderate to severe IDD are often <b>relieved</b> to find a practice that can passively stabilize their loved one instead of asking them to hold the patient. Some prefer IV sedation, but sedation is riskier in this population (fragile health, airway problems). Every family is different: talk with them.</p>\n<p><b>Active or passive stabilization requires informed consent.</b> Having a staff member hold a patient still needs the same consent as a papoose board. Without consent a dentist risks a <b>criminal charge (assault and battery)</b> and a <b>personal tort (malpractice)</b>. Good communication protects you: Dr. Mitchell checks in with parents during treatment and tells them they \"have their foot on the brake.\"</p>\n<p>Consent may be <b>express</b> (given in words or writing that unambiguously shows intent) or <b>implied</b> (a person's actions raise the presumption of consent: an adult who walks in for an exam and cleaning). Routine care an average person expects may be implied; beyond that, seek express consent.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Express</th><th>Implied</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>How it's given</td><td>In words or in writing, unambiguously</td><td>By the person's actions or conduct</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>A signed, specific consent for a papoose board</td><td>An adult who comes in for an exam and cleaning</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Covers</td><td>Treatment beyond routine care, and any stabilization</td><td>Routine care an average person expects</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Informed consent needs <b>three elements</b>:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Mental capacity</b> to make reasonable decisions (of whoever gives consent)</li><li><b>Informed</b>: enough information to make an educated decision. A <b>blanket consent</b> signed at registration is meaningless, because the patient hasn't been told their needs or options.</li><li><b>Given voluntarily</b>: if there is hesitation, it's best not to proceed.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Document it</b>: written and specific in the chart; witnessed, dated and recorded; with a way to record <b>withdrawal</b> of consent. Suggested items: date of use, <b>justification</b> (often overlooked: unable to tolerate treatment safely, resisting care, biting instruments, staff at risk), description of the restraint (Molt prop, Rainbow board, head hold), its effectiveness, its duration, and periodic re-evaluation.</p>\n<h4>Ethics of treating patients with IDD</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Issue one: is the patient competent to take part in decisions?</b> A competent patient has the right to decline care, even a bad decision. Include patients in decisions whenever possible. (Group homes in Alabama often say a patient who barely speaks in sentences consents for themselves.)</li><li><b>Issue two: if the patient is not competent, how do we proceed?</b> By <b>beneficence</b>: act to maximize the patient's well-being. Is a composite placed in a wet, bleeding field to avoid stabilization better for the patient than stabilizing them and placing a quality restoration?</li><li><b>Issue three: advocacy.</b> This population can't speak for itself. Educate caregivers on the best treatment, and push for resources at the community, state and national level. How we care for them reflects our values as a society.</li></ul>\n<p>Not using stabilization when it's needed has ethical consequences too, just as using it wrongly does.</p>\n","down":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>How Down syndrome occurs: nondisjunction and the three types, with their percentages</li><li>Incidence, maternal age and life expectancy</li><li>Intellect, aging and Alzheimer's changes; personality and behavior</li><li>Facial features: midface hypoplasia, nose, ears, eyes</li><li>Medical conditions: heart defects and prophylaxis, immunity and leukemia, thyroid, diabetes, celiac, airway, dysphagia, GERD, atlantoaxial instability</li><li>Oral findings, periodontal disease and NUG, caries</li><li>Treatment planning: prevention, perio, extractions, liquid antibiotics, prosthodontics, restorations</li></ul></div>\n<p>This is one of Dr. Mitchell's favorite topics and patient groups. Much of it comes from Dr. John Thornton, who taught this course for many years. The goals: explain how Down syndrome occurs, anticipate behavior, identify medical conditions, describe oral findings and plan treatment for the common oral diseases.</p>\n<h4>What is Down syndrome?</h4>\n<p>Described and named by <b>Dr. Langdon Down in 1865</b>; recognized as a <b>chromosomal anomaly in 1956</b>, almost 100 years later. Normal cells have <b>46 chromosomes</b>; most people with Down syndrome have <b>47</b>, with an extra copy of chromosome 21 (<b>trisomy 21</b>). It is a syndrome, not a disease.</p>\n<p>The cause is <b>nondisjunction</b>. In normal meiosis the 23 pairs split so each gamete gets 23 unpaired chromosomes. In Down syndrome the 21st pair doesn't split: one gamete lacks chromosome 21 and isn't viable; the other has 24. If it is fertilized, the cell has 47 chromosomes, and every division copies that. <b>Over 90%</b> of the time the error is in the <b>mother's egg</b>; <b>under 10%</b> in the father's sperm.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Trisomy 21 (94%)</b>: an extra whole chromosome 21; 47 chromosomes.</li><li><b>Translocation (5%)</b>: part of an extra 21 attaches to another chromosome, usually the <b>14th</b>. The person has <b>46</b> chromosomes, one of them unusually long.</li><li><b>Mosaicism (1%)</b>: nondisjunction happens early in the <b>embryo's</b> cell divisions, not in the gamete, so some cells have 46 and some 47, like the tiles of a mosaic.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Share</th><th>What happens</th><th>Chromosomes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Trisomy 21</td><td>94%</td><td>Nondisjunction in a gamete: an extra whole chromosome 21</td><td>47</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Translocation</td><td>5%</td><td>Extra chromosome 21 material attached to another chromosome, usually 14</td><td>46</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mosaicism</td><td>1%</td><td>Nondisjunction early in the embryo, not in the gamete</td><td>Some cells 46, some 47</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>How common</h4>\n<p>About 250,000 people in the U.S. in 2008 (surely more now), about <b>1 in 700</b> live births. Incidence <b>rises with maternal age</b>: about 1 in 2,300 at 20, 1 in 280 at 34-39, 1 in 130 at 40-44 and 1 in 65 at 46. More educated families tend to start families later, so Down syndrome can be more common in more highly educated communities.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mother's age</th><th>Births with Down syndrome</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>About 20</td><td>1 in 2,300</td></tr>\n<tr><td>34-39</td><td>1 in 280</td></tr>\n<tr><td>40-44</td><td>1 in 130</td></tr>\n<tr><td>46 and over</td><td>1 in 65</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overall</td><td>About 1 in 700</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Life expectancy</b> has risen dramatically: <b>9 years in 1929</b> (most died of pneumonia before antibiotics), <b>30 in 1980</b>, and <b>55 by the 2000s</b>, now even more.</p>\n<h4>Intellect and aging</h4>\n<p>Most have intellectual disability: <b>42% mild</b>, <b>26% moderate</b> (so about 68%, nearly 70%, mild or moderate and able to function well in society), <b>15% severe</b> and <b>17% profound</b>. About <b>half show signs of deterioration before age 50</b>. In <b>100% of autopsies</b>, the brain shows changes associated with <b>Alzheimer's</b>, though the clinical signs are not as evident. Because long lives are new, the data are only 15-20 years deep. A patient you have seen for 25 years may start to understand or behave differently as they near 50: expect it.</p>\n<h4>Personality</h4>\n<p>Generally <b>happy, playful and loving</b> (\"the best hugs you will ever receive\"). But emotions can <b>shift quickly</b>: they scare easily and get upset easily (a laughing patient became terrified the moment she realized there would be a needle). They are <b>sensitive to the emotions around them</b>: a tense room makes them tense, a playful room playful. <b>As intellectual disability increases, behavior worsens</b>: lower-functioning patients are driven more by mistrust, may <b>withdraw from strangers</b>, and can be <b>violently protective</b>. Dr. Mitchell's example: a man with Down syndrome, autism and profound ID whose family trimmed his nails while he slept.</p>\n<h4>Facial features</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Midface hypoplasia</b>: the <b>cardinal characteristic</b>. The maxillary complex fails to develop fully, giving a sunken-in, flat face, most obvious from the side.</li><li><b>Reduced nasal protrusion</b>: a flat, broad nasal bridge (59-78%), a <b>saddle nose</b>.</li><li><b>Deviated nasal septum and conchae</b>, which narrow the airway. With the midface and nose, the result is <b>mouth breathing</b>. Fill that mouth with water spray and you have cut off the airway: the \"bad behavior\" is a fight for air. The same anatomy makes the airway easier to lose under sedation.</li><li><b>Ear malformations (~50%)</b>: low-set ears, lop ear (the top rim folded over), a flat or absent helix.</li><li><b>Eyes</b>: <b>epicanthal folds</b> (skin of the upper eyelid covering the inner corner; slanting, almond-shaped eyes; 78%), <b>strabismus</b> (14-54%), <b>nystagmus</b> (involuntary eye movement), refractive error (50% wear glasses; one reference says 100% see worse than normal). Low ears, saddle nose and poor eyesight make glasses hard to fit (Specs4Us makes frames with a lower bridge).</li></ul>\n<h4>Medical considerations: the heart</h4>\n<p>About <b>50%</b> have congenital heart disease, so the cardiac history matters most.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Atrioventricular canal defect</b> (also AV septal or endocardial cushion defect): the <b>most common</b>, 45-60% of the cardiac cases; a large wall defect involving atria and ventricles, so oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mix. Usually repaired early; about six months after a good repair it rarely affects dental care. If the repair left poor flow, antibiotics may be needed: consult the cardiologist.</li><li><b>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</b>: 32-35%; a smaller hole between the ventricles; repaired early.</li><li><b>Atrial septal defect (ASD)</b>: 1-8%; a hole between the atria.</li><li><b>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</b>: 7%; the ductus arteriosus normally closes within hours of birth; if it stays open, oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood mix. Easily closed surgically.</li><li><b>Tetralogy of Fallot</b>: 4-6%; a VSD, <b>pulmonary stenosis</b>, a thicker right ventricle, and an <b>aorta over the VSD</b>. Cyanosis, rapid breathing, fainting spells. Needs open-heart surgery, often isn't fully repaired, and <b>often requires prophylactic antibiotics</b>: get a cardiac consult.</li><li><b>Mitral valve prolapse</b>: 14% by medical history but <b>57% on EKG</b>; <b>aortic regurgitation</b>: 6% by history, 11% on EKG. <b>Patients often don't know their own cardiac disease.</b></li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Defect</th><th>Share of cardiac cases</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Atrioventricular canal defect (AV septal, endocardial cushion)</td><td>45-60%</td><td>Most common; atria and ventricles; repaired early</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</td><td>32-35%</td><td>Repaired early; usually no antibiotics once healed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Atrial septal defect (ASD)</td><td>1-8%</td><td>Repaired early</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</td><td>7%</td><td>The duct fails to close after birth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tetralogy of Fallot</td><td>4-6%</td><td>VSD, pulmonary stenosis, thick right ventricle, aorta over the VSD; cyanosis; often needs prophylaxis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitral valve prolapse</td><td>14% by history, 57% by EKG</td><td>Patients often don't know</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aortic regurgitation</td><td>6% by history, 11% by EKG</td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Antibiotic prophylaxis</b> is now only for those at highest risk of endocarditis. It's required for: (1) <b>cyanotic congenital heart disease not fully repaired</b>, including surgical shunts and conduits; (2) a congenital defect <b>completely repaired with prosthetic material or a device, for the first six months</b> after repair; (3) <b>repaired</b> congenital heart disease <b>with residual defects</b> at or next to a prosthetic patch or device. It's often good to consult the physician.</p>\n<h4>Immunity and leukemia</h4>\n<p>A <b>smaller thymus</b>, fewer B and T cells, impaired T-cell function and impaired chemotaxis. The results: more <b>upper respiratory infections</b> (more mouth breathing and speech problems, harder general anesthesia), more <b>otitis media</b> (ear infections and early antibiotics correlate with molar-incisor hypomineralization), and higher risk of <b>pneumonia</b>, which caused most early deaths before antibiotics.</p>\n<p><b>Leukemia is 15-50 times more common.</b> Myeloid leukemia (called AML in others, <b>Down syndrome myeloid leukemia</b> here) has a <b>cure rate around 80%</b>. Lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL; mostly B-cell precursors) used to respond poorly to the usual methotrexate and anthracyclines, but newer treatment has nearly matched the general cure rate. Oddly, <b>solid tumors</b> are not more common and may be less common.</p>\n<h4>Endocrine problems</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Thyroid</b>: hypothyroidism <b>28-35 times</b> more common in infants; TSH raised in 25-60%; symptoms hard to diagnose; <b>autoimmune hypothyroidism</b> in 7.5-34% of children 8 and older, with <b>equal male and female</b> rates (mostly female in others).</li><li><b>Diabetes</b>: <b>3 times</b> more likely; type 1 starts slightly younger.</li><li><b>Celiac disease</b>: immune-mediated, triggered by gluten; up to <b>20%</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Airway, swallowing and the neck</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Obstructive sleep apnea</b>: <b>50-80%</b>.</li><li><b>Dysphagia</b> (abnormal swallowing): <b>58%</b>; risk of aspirating liquids, food and saliva. About <b>60% have GERD</b>, so they can aspirate stomach contents too. Watch positioning (don't lay them too far back), don't flood the mouth, suction well. Be cautious with sedation; monitor more and use the hospital more often.</li><li><b>Atlantoaxial instability</b>: <b>10-20%</b>. Lax ligaments let C1 and C2 move too far, risking spinal cord injury. <b>Avoid hyperextending the neck</b>, especially during stabilization or intubation. It may lessen with age.</li></ul>\n<h4>Oral findings</h4>\n<p>A <b>narrow palate</b> and <b>spaces between teeth</b>. <b>Class III</b> malocclusion in 30-70%, from the underdeveloped <b>maxilla</b> (midface hypoplasia), not a large mandible; Class II in 3-32%; <b>posterior crossbite in 71%</b>; open bite in 5%. <b>Macroglossia</b> (protect and work around the tongue) and a <b>fissured tongue</b>. <b>Delayed eruption</b> of primary and permanent teeth, more <b>impacted canines and premolars</b>, <b>over-retained primary teeth</b>, and <b>peg or conical teeth</b>. More <b>missing teeth</b>: laterals missing in <b>35-43%</b> vs 2% generally. Short, small clinical crowns (<b>microdontia</b>) and <b>taurodontism</b>: the root bifurcation and pulp chamber floor displaced apically, giving large pulp chambers.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Malocclusion</td><td>Class III 30-70% (midface hypoplasia, not a big mandible); Class II 3-32%; posterior crossbite 71%; open bite 5%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Palate and spacing</td><td>Narrow palate; spaces between teeth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tongue</td><td>Macroglossia; fissured tongue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eruption</td><td>Delayed (primary and permanent); impacted canines and premolars; over-retained primary teeth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tooth number and shape</td><td>Missing laterals 35-43% (vs 2%); peg or conical teeth; microdontia (short crowns); taurodontism</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Periodontal disease</td><td>The most common dental condition: early onset in 60-95%, from age 6, faster</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NUG</td><td>45% (vs 4% of others with IDD), without the usual fetid breath or pain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Caries</td><td>Usually lower (69-78% caries free in studies), but the evidence is mixed</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Periodontal disease</h4>\n<p><b>The most common dental condition</b> in Down syndrome: higher than the general population and higher than in other disabilities, so poor brushing alone doesn't explain it. <b>Early onset in 60-95%</b>, detectable as young as <b>6 years</b>, and it progresses faster. Some patients lose teeth to bone loss in their early teens. Treatment mirrors early-onset periodontitis: scaling and root planing.</p>\n<p><b>Necrotizing ulcerative gingivitis (NUG)</b>: an institutional study found it in <b>45%</b> of people with Down syndrome vs <b>4%</b> of others with IDD, and they rarely report the fetid breath or pain others do. Predisposing factors: crowding (rare in Down syndrome), traumatic occlusion, peg-shaped anterior teeth, primary teeth whose roots don't resorb, and a high frenum.</p>\n<h4>Caries</h4>\n<p>Commonly believed to be <b>lower</b>: one study found 72-78% caries free vs 46-58% of their siblings; another 69% vs 45% of others with IDD. But the <i>International Journal of Paediatric Dentistry</i> (2016) said there is no scientific evidence that people with Down syndrome have less caries. Dr. Mitchell's experience: lower, but he has seen plenty of caries.</p>\n<h4>Treatment</h4>\n<p><b>Prevention</b> is worth a pound of cure. Start early and tell families <b>perio is the big risk</b>. Parents should brush for the child: let the child \"play\" at brushing, then go behind to really clean. Parents keep that job much later in life than usual. Larger handles and flossing aids (a flosser whose floss runs perpendicular to the handle reaches the molars) help.</p>\n<p><b>Start periodontal therapy early</b>: probe from about <b>age 6</b>, look for bone loss on every film, and do SRP as for anyone.</p>\n<p><b>Pharmacologic</b>: long-term <b>chlorhexidine</b> (staining is an issue and spitting may be limited; dip the toothbrush and brush it on). Antibiotics: <b>penicillin</b>; <b>doxycycline (Periostat)</b> in low dose <b>after 8-10 years of age</b> (no longer a staining risk to developing teeth); <b>metronidazole</b>; local <b>PerioChip or Atridox</b> in a pocket.</p>\n<p><b>Extractions in perio treatment</b> are a complicated decision. A patient who can't tolerate complex SRP favors extracting; a patient who won't tolerate a prosthesis favors keeping teeth; crowding favors extraction, but many have spacing, which favors keeping. Decide with the caregivers. Healing after extraction is likely delayed; antibiotics are often recommended, but that is <b>not evidence-driven</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Antibiotic prescriptions</b>: if the patient can swallow pills, write it as for anyone. If not, use a <b>liquid</b>: find the highest readily available concentration and prescribe a volume close to the pill dose. Dr. Mitchell's example, 1,500 mg of amoxicillin a day for 5 days:</p>\n<ul><li>Amoxicillin 500 mg tablets, disp 15, one tablet by mouth TID for 5 days</li><li>Amoxicillin <b>250 mg/5 mL</b>, disp <b>150 mL</b>, take <b>10 mL</b> by mouth TID for 5 days (10 mL = 500 mg)</li><li>Amoxicillin <b>400 mg/5 mL</b>, disp <b>75 mL</b>, take <b>1 teaspoon (5 mL)</b> by mouth TID for 5 days (a little less, but less liquid to swallow)</li></ul>\n<p><b>Prosthetic treatment</b> is often of limited value: periodontal problems, ability to clean, Class III occlusion, impaired healing and infection control (implants), and above all maladaptive behavior, fear and gag reflex. Dr. Mitchell doesn't know a patient with Down syndrome who tolerates a denture or RPD, but assess each person and explain risks and benefits.</p>\n<p><b>Restorative care</b>: high-functioning, cooperative patients are planned like anyone. <b>As ID increases, favor strong over esthetic</b>: full-coverage stainless steel crowns over large complex restorations, posterior amalgam over resin, and resin-modified glass ionomer (fluoride release, easy placement).</p>\n<p>Summary: people with Down syndrome are <b>often wonderful patients</b> and probably the most common developmental disability treated in private practice. They have unique physical features, medical conditions to check (heart, infection), and periodontal disease that demands prevention.</p>\n","cp":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>What CP is, its causes (prenatal, neonatal, postnatal, TORCH) and prevalence</li><li>Types of CP and their movements; the limb terms</li><li>Intellect: most have normal or near-normal cognition</li><li>Medical conditions: epilepsy and seizure management, status epilepticus, medications (Dilantin, Depakote, bisphosphonates), dysphagia, GERD, drooling, dysarthria</li><li>Consent in a patient who can't speak well</li><li>Oral findings and treatment; g-tube calculus</li><li>Home care, wheelchair transfer and positioning</li></ul></div>\n<p>Dr. Mitchell's cerebral palsy lecture (with Dr. John Thornton) comes in four sections: introduction and types, medical conditions, oral health, and prevention with wheelchair care. Goals: describe causes and types, obtain consent, identify oral conditions, manage the airway and gag reflex, plan treatment, and care for a patient in a wheelchair.</p>\n<h4>What CP is</h4>\n<p>A <b>neuromuscular disorder affecting voluntary muscle movement and posture</b>. It is <b>permanent</b> (they won't grow out of it) and <b>non-progressive</b> (a child with stiff legs and normal hands will be a teenager with stiff legs and normal hands). It is caused in the <b>first years of life</b> by abnormal development or <b>damage to the brain</b> before birth, during delivery, or soon after. An injury at 5 or 15 may mimic CP, but it isn't CP.</p>\n<h4>How common</h4>\n<p>The <b>most common motor disorder in children</b>: about <b>1 in 303</b> children, 0.2% of live births. Risk rises as <b>birth weight falls</b> (59 per 1,000 under 3.3 lb vs 1.3 per 1,000 over 5.5 lb) and as <b>gestation shortens</b> (43 per 1,000 before 28 weeks vs 1.3 per 1,000 at 37 weeks or more). Among 8-year-olds (CDC), the average was 3.1 per 1,000; Alabama 3.2, Georgia highest at 3.6.</p>\n<h4>Causes</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Prenatal: 70-80%</b>. Prematurity; intrauterine infections; viral infections (about 5% of CP), notably the <b>TORCH</b> infections (<b>To</b>xoplasmosis, <b>R</b>ubella, <b>C</b>ytomegalovirus, <b>H</b>erpes), a term Dr. Mitchell \"could see popping up on a board\"; <b>multiple embryos</b> (twins, triplets); intracranial hemorrhage; radiation and drugs.</li><li><b>Neonatal: 10-28%</b>. <b>Hypoxia</b> (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy) was once thought the main cause; it is now known to be a small share. Mechanical ventilation of premature babies; trouble regulating blood pressure.</li><li><b>Postnatal: 5-18%</b> (closer to 5%). Trauma in the first years (<b>shaken baby syndrome</b>, abusive head trauma, <b>drowning</b>), infections (encephalitis, meningitis), toxins, brain tumors.</li></ul>\n<h4>Types</h4>\n<p><b>Spastic CP (70-80%)</b>: <b>cerebral cortex</b> damage reduces motor control. Muscles are <b>stiff</b>, almost constantly contracted, with <b>hypertonicity</b> (increased tone: a wiry, strong build), <b>hyperreflexia</b> (overreactive reflexes) and <b>contractures</b> (muscles and tendons lose elasticity, limiting joint motion and curling the hands). <b>Startling stimuli</b>, such as loud noises, can trigger spasms.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Limbs involved</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Hemiplegia</td><td>Arm and leg on the same side</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quadriplegia</td><td>All four, arms worse than in diplegia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diplegia</td><td>Legs, with minimal arm involvement</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Monoplegia</td><td>One limb (rare)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Paraplegia</td><td>Only the legs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Triplegia</td><td>Any three</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Dyskinetic CP (10-20%)</b>: lesions in the <b>basal ganglia</b> cause <b>involuntary, non-rhythmic</b> movements of three kinds: <b>athetosis</b> (slow, uncontrolled twisting and writhing), <b>dystonic tremor</b> (mixed tone: opposing muscles contract at once, so the shake comes with trying to move, unlike Parkinson's rest tremor), and <b>chorea</b> (fast movements).</p>\n<p><b>Ataxic CP (5-10%)</b>: a <b>cerebellum</b> lesion: <b>lack of coordination</b>, poor balance when walking (walkers are common), poor hand-eye coordination, intention tremors.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Share</th><th>Brain area</th><th>Movement</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Spastic</td><td>70-80%</td><td>Cerebral cortex</td><td>Stiff muscles: hypertonicity, hyperreflexia, contractures; startled by loud noises</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dyskinetic</td><td>10-20%</td><td>Basal ganglia</td><td>Involuntary movements: athetosis (slow writhing), dystonic tremor, chorea (fast)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ataxic</td><td>5-10%</td><td>Cerebellum</td><td>Poor coordination and balance, intention tremor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mixed</td><td></td><td>More than one area</td><td>A mix</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Intellect: \"maybe my most important slide\"</h4>\n<p><b>Most people with CP have normal or near-normal intellect.</b> About <b>30-50% have normal cognition</b>, about <b>45% mild ID</b>, and only about <b>15% severe ID</b>. ID is more common with greater motor involvement and in spastic CP. <b>Never assume a cognitive disability</b> because someone struggles to speak: Daniel, the class case, is as smart as anyone in the room.</p>\n<h4>Medical conditions: epilepsy</h4>\n<p><b>Epilepsy</b> in about <b>30%</b> of people with CP vs about 2.5% of the general population; <b>tonic-clonic</b> seizures are most common. It raises the risk of dental trauma and of a seizure during treatment, and the medications affect care.</p>\n<p><b>Managing a seizure in the chair</b>: <b>Stop</b> the procedure; <b>Remove</b> everything from the mouth; turn the patient on their <b>Side</b> (so vomit isn't aspirated); make sure they are <b>Safe</b> (nothing to hit, can't fall); <b>Allow</b> the seizure to run its course, which should end in under five minutes; then complete only urgent treatment (temporize) and <b>Release</b> them home, exhausted.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Stop</td><td>Stop the procedure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Remove</td><td>Take everything out of the mouth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Side</td><td>Turn the patient on the side (aspiration)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Safe</td><td>Keep them from falling or hitting anything</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Allow</td><td>Let it run its course: it should end in under 5 minutes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Release</td><td>Finish only urgent work, then send them home</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Status epilepticus</b> is the emergency: a single seizure <b>lasting more than five minutes</b>, or <b>two or more within five minutes</b> without returning to normal between them. Activate <b>EMS</b>, monitor the airway and breathing (oxygen), and if the patient collapses, provide <b>BLS</b> until EMS arrives.</p>\n<p><b>Dental trauma</b> from seizures (fractures, avulsions) is treated much as in anyone, but factor in tolerance: can a reimplanted avulsed tooth's root canal be done? Can complex resin restorations be maintained?</p>\n<h4>Medications</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Dilantin (phenytoin)</b>: <b>gingival hyperplasia</b>. Not used much anymore; most newer drugs don't cause it. Consult the physician about switching; frequent cleanings and excellent home care; refer to a periodontist periodically for resection. If hygiene can't be controlled (often), <b>delay</b> resection, because the tissue grows back fast. Send when there is a risk or occurrence of <b>periodontal infection</b>, or the tissue <b>grows over the occlusal surfaces</b>. Methods: modified flap (CP patients can't manage periodontal packing), electrocautery, lasers; it often needs sedation.</li><li><b>Depakote (valproic acid)</b>: risk of <b>thrombocytopenia</b> and bleeding.</li><li><b>Bisphosphonates</b> (for osteoporosis or osteopenia in CP): risk of <b>osteonecrosis</b> of alveolar bone; caution with extractions, consult the physician, lean toward the oral surgeon.</li></ul>\n<h4>Dysphagia, GERD and drooling</h4>\n<p><b>Dysphagia</b>: abnormal swallowing with a delayed reflex, giving a <b>sensitive gag reflex</b> and coughing, choking and aspiration risk. Protect the airway: often <b>don't lay them back fully</b>, suction often, and use water <b>intermittently</b> in short bursts. They are not being uncooperative; they can't swallow the liquid.</p>\n<p><b>GERD</b> in <b>over 50%</b>, and many can't say they have heartburn. <b>Dental erosion may be the first sign</b>: a <b>scooped-out</b> appearance, unlike the <b>flat</b> wear of bruxism. Refer to the physician. If the teeth are symptomatic, restore; if not, <b>don't restore until after the GERD is resolved</b> (it may never need it, and restoring sooner just fails).</p>\n<p><b>Drooling</b> in <b>10-40%</b>, caused by <b>dysphagia</b>, <b>not hypersalivation</b>. It carries stigma (bibs, towels). Reducing saliva should be a <b>last resort</b>: <b>Botox</b> injections (wear off), <b>anticholinergics</b> (scopolamine, glycopyrrolate), or surgery (ligating the parotid duct, excising the submandibular gland). There is no clear evidence it helps, and the dry mouth can cause <b>rampant caries and tooth loss</b>. Saliva is protecting the patient.</p>\n<h4>Speech and consent</h4>\n<p><b>Dysarthria</b> (difficulty articulating) is present in up to <b>88%</b>: slow, poorly pronounced words. It does <b>not</b> indicate cognitive capacity. Always speak to a person with CP assuming normal cognition. Some use speech devices (Daniel types with a head wand; tablets now do the same far more cheaply).</p>\n<p>Since most have normal or near-normal cognition, <b>most consent to their own treatment</b>: some can sign; most can give <b>verbal consent witnessed</b> by a second party; others use a code (one blink = yes, looking down = no) or a communication device. Only if low functioning does a caregiver consent. <b>Unless very low functioning, they have the right to refuse treatment.</b></p>\n<h4>Oral and facial features</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Long, narrow face (dolichofacial)</b>: weak muscles allow excess vertical growth; the palatal, occlusal and mandibular planes are <b>hyperdivergent</b>.</li><li><b>Open bite</b>, maintained (not caused) by <b>tongue thrust</b>.</li><li><b>Flared (procumbent) incisors</b> and <b>increased overjet</b>, with <b>mandibular retrusion</b>.</li><li><b>Class II</b> malocclusion, usually <b>skeletal</b> (retrognathic mandible).</li><li><b>Trauma</b>: unprotected incisors (overjet, procumbent) plus falls (poor balance) and epilepsy mean more <b>avulsions and incisor fractures</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>Orthodontics is often not tolerated: appliances, monthly visits, the gag reflex, and caries around brackets can outweigh the benefit. No contraindication beyond tolerance and hygiene.</p>\n<h4>Caries, perio and wear</h4>\n<p><b>Caries</b> rates are a little higher: poor hygiene, <b>soft diet</b>, <b>enamel hypoplasia</b>, <b>mouth breathing</b>, <b>anti-drooling medications</b>, and food held in the mouth longer because of dysphagia. Treatment: fast-set alginate if you take impressions (digital impressions may open doors); <b>stainless steel crowns</b> instead of cast crowns; resin only with good isolation; <b>RMGI</b> where not in function; <b>amalgam</b> where function matters.</p>\n<p><b>Periodontal disease</b> is up to <b>3 times more common</b>, but there is <b>no more calculus</b> than usual: it's plaque that isn't removed. The exception is a patient fed by <b>g-tube</b>, who builds <b>heavy calculus</b>: remove it (often ultrasonic) from the buccal and lingual, but <b>leave it on the occlusal table</b>, where it protects. Regular cleanings (3 months, even monthly), hand or ultrasonic scaling with controlled water, cleaning in pulses, and long-term <b>chlorhexidine</b> (staining) <b>brushed on</b> with a dipped toothbrush for 30-60 seconds, since many can't rinse and spit.</p>\n<p><b>Bruxism</b> in <b>58%</b>: flat wear (not scooped); <b>pulp exposures are rare</b> because wear is slow; worse with lower muscle and cognitive function. <b>Erosion and hypoplasia</b> in <b>25-35%</b> (GERD, developmental): hypoplastic enamel attracts plaque and calculus, is lost faster, can be sensitive, and may need full coverage sooner.</p>\n<p><b>What's normal in CP</b>: the number of teeth (no more missing or extra), eruption timing, and tongue size.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Face</td><td>Long and narrow (dolichofacial): weak muscles, hyperdivergent planes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Malocclusion</td><td>Open bite (kept open by tongue thrust), flared incisors, more overjet, Class II with a retrusive mandible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trauma</td><td>Avulsions and incisor fractures: unprotected incisors plus falls and seizures</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Caries</td><td>A little higher: hygiene, soft diet, hypoplasia, mouth breathing, anti-drooling drugs, food held in the mouth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Periodontal disease</td><td>Up to 3x more common; no more calculus, except g-tube patients</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bruxism</td><td>58%; flat wear; pulp exposures rare</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Erosion and hypoplasia</td><td>25-35%; GERD and developmental</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Normal</td><td>Number of teeth, eruption times, tongue size</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Prevention and home care</h4>\n<p>Prevention is key. Many lack the hand-eye coordination to brush; those living alone may have no help; group homes and families help inconsistently, so educate caregivers and modify the aids. Commercial options: fat-handled brushes, the <b>Surround</b> three-headed toothbrush, the Reach flosser, hand straps. Homemade: push the brush through a <b>tennis ball</b>, wrap <b>foam</b> or a <b>washcloth with rubber bands</b> around the handle (or putty, acrylic); <b>lengthen</b> the handle with tongue blades for a patient who can't bring the hand close to the mouth.</p>\n<p><b>What doesn't help</b>: <b>Waterpik</b>, <b>disclosing tablets</b> (messy with drooling and poor rinsing), and <b>mouth rinses</b> (most can't rinse and spit).</p>\n<h4>Wheelchairs</h4>\n<p>Many arrive in wheelchairs. If moving them to the dental chair, <b>let the caregivers transfer them</b>. Lifts help: ceiling lifts, electric or manual hydraulic lifts (the harness stays under the patient). You can also <b>treat from the wheelchair</b>: back it up next to the dental chair, <b>lock the wheels</b> (a 300-pound electric chair can roll onto your foot), and use the tilt mechanism. You can't raise or lower the patient. You may need to leave the patient at about <b>45 degrees</b> because of dysphagia.</p>\n<p>Summary: CP is non-progressive and present from birth; muscle control is most affected; most have normal or near-normal cognition, and they understand far more than they can say; guard the airway and limit water; prevention is key.</p>\n","syndromes":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Descriptive terms: the -dactylies, eye terms, glossoptosis, spine curves, meningocele, dolichocephaly vs brachycephaly</li><li>Fragile X: the gene, why boys, the features</li><li>Prader-Willi: the deletion, appetite, behavior, dental concerns</li><li>Tuberous sclerosis: findings depend on where the tumors are</li><li>Apert syndrome: craniosynostosis and its oral features</li><li>Lesch-Nyhan: self-injury and full-mouth extractions</li><li>Pierre Robin sequence: the airway</li><li>Fetal alcohol syndrome and Cornelia de Lange: faces and behavior</li></ul></div>\n<p>Instead of one common condition, this lecture covers several less common syndromes and the terms used to describe them. You won't remember them all: keep the reference reading handy and learn to <b>look things up quickly</b>, which Dr. Mitchell calls an incredibly important skill in this field.</p>\n<h4>Terms for the hands</h4>\n<p><b>-dactyly</b> always means fingers or toes. <b>Brachydactyly</b>: <b>short</b> fingers (like bradycardia is slow, brachy is short). <b>Syndactyly</b>: an abnormal <b>fusion</b> of the bones of the hands or feet (the right number, joined together). <b>Clinodactyly</b>: a permanent <b>deflection</b> (bend) of one or more fingers. <b>Ectrodactyly</b>: congenital <b>absence</b> of one or more fingers or toes (bones actually missing). <b>Polydactyly</b>: <b>more than five</b> digits on a hand or foot, sometimes just a small extra appendage.</p>\n<h4>Terms for the eyes</h4>\n<p><b>Coloboma</b>: a <b>hole</b> in a structure of the eye (iris, retina, choroid or optic disc); in syndromes it usually means the iris, the one a dentist can see. <b>Miosis</b>: a <b>constricted</b> pupil; <b>mydriasis</b>: a <b>dilated</b> pupil. <b>Strabismus</b>: an eye out of alignment, \"cross-eyed.\" <b>Hypertelorism</b>: an abnormal distance between two paired organs, usually <b>wide-set eyes</b>: in the embryo the eyes start wide and move toward the midline, and if facial development stops early they stay apart.</p>\n<h4>Tongue, spine and face</h4>\n<p><b>Glossoptosis</b>: the tongue is displaced <b>downward and retracted</b>. It can get in the way of the palatal shelves and cause <b>cleft palate</b>, and it can block the <b>airway</b>. A feature of the <b>Pierre Robin sequence</b> and Down syndrome.</p>\n<p><b>Scoliosis</b>: the spine curves <b>sideways</b>, <b>C-shaped</b> (the head ends near the midline) or <b>S-shaped</b> (the head ends off to the side). <b>Kyphosis</b>: the spine curves with convexity <b>backward</b>, bending the patient forward. Both, especially kyphosis, mean the head won't reach the headrest: support it with pillows.</p>\n<p><b>Meningomyelocele</b>: the meninges <b>and part of the spinal cord</b> herniate through an opening in the spine (as in spina bifida), seen as a bulge on the back. <b>Meningocele</b>: only the <b>meninges</b> herniate, with fluid.</p>\n<p><b>Dolichocephaly</b> (like dolichofacial): a <b>long, narrow face</b>, with the palatal, occlusal and mandibular planes <b>diverging</b> (hyperdivergent): such a child keeps growing longer. <b>Brachycephaly</b>: a <b>short, square face</b> with a strong, <b>flat jaw line</b> and nearly parallel planes; less common in syndromes.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Brachydactyly</td><td>Short fingers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Syndactyly</td><td>Fused fingers or toes (the right number of bones, joined)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Clinodactyly</td><td>Permanent deflection (bend) of one or more fingers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ectrodactyly</td><td>Congenital absence of one or more fingers or toes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Polydactyly</td><td>More than five digits on a hand or foot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coloboma</td><td>A hole in a structure of the eye (iris, retina, choroid, optic disc); usually the iris</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Miosis / mydriasis</td><td>Constricted pupil / dilated pupil</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Strabismus</td><td>Misaligned eye: cross-eyed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hypertelorism</td><td>Abnormal distance between paired organs; usually wide-set eyes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glossoptosis</td><td>Tongue displaced downward and retracted; can cause cleft palate; Pierre Robin and Down syndrome</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scoliosis</td><td>Spine curved sideways (C-shaped or S-shaped)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kyphosis</td><td>Spine curved with convexity backward (bent forward)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Meningomyelocele / meningocele</td><td>Herniation with spinal cord / meninges only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dolichocephaly</td><td>Long, narrow face; hyperdivergent planes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brachycephaly</td><td>Short, square face; strong, flat jaw line; parallel planes</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Fragile X syndrome</h4>\n<p>A genetic syndrome from changes in the <b>FMR1</b> gene on the <b>X chromosome</b>: a small part of the code is <b>repeated</b> over and over, and the more repeats, the more likely the symptoms. <b>Most common in boys</b>: with one X, an affected X shows fully (a <b>dosing effect</b>). Girls have two Xs, only one active; if the affected X is the one turned off, there are few or no symptoms, and if the healthy one is turned off, a girl can show the disease.</p>\n<p>Features: <b>delayed crawling and walking</b>, <b>hand flapping</b> and <b>avoiding eye contact</b> (autistic behaviors; some consider it part of the autism spectrum), hyperactivity or impulsiveness, mental delay, speech delay, a <b>long face with a prominent jaw</b>, and flat feet. Dr. Thornton liked to point out that some have suggested Billy the Kid had fragile X. Resource: the National Fragile X Foundation.</p>\n<h4>Prader-Willi syndrome</h4>\n<p>Caused by a <b>deletion</b> of function of part of <b>chromosome 15 (15q)</b>. Normally only the <b>paternal</b> copy of these genes is turned on and the maternal copy is silent. In <b>70%</b> of cases the critical paternal genes are <b>deleted</b>, and the maternal copy, though present, is not turned on. Most cases are <b>new mutations</b>, not inherited.</p>\n<p>The hallmark is an <b>insatiable appetite</b>, leading to <b>obesity</b> (families lock the refrigerator). <b>Mild to moderate</b> intellectual impairment, similar to Down syndrome, but different behavior: <b>temper outbursts, stubbornness and compulsive behavior</b> (patients with Down syndrome tend to be passive). They may be <b>insensitive to pain</b>: procedures can be easier, but a tooth that would hurt anyone else may go unreported. Physical features: obesity, <b>poor muscle tone</b> (feeding difficulties, food held in the mouth), narrow forehead, almond-shaped eyes, a <b>triangular mouth</b> (thin upper lip, downturned corners), short stature, small hands and feet.</p>\n<p><b>Dental concerns</b>: obesity makes <b>sedation</b> risky (general anesthesia may be safer than deep sedation; watch the airway); behavioral outbursts (many are very manageable); <b>decreased, viscous saliva</b>, so <b>heavy calculus</b>, more <b>caries</b> (overeating adds to it) and <b>gingivitis</b>; a <b>high-arched palate</b>.</p>\n<h4>Tuberous sclerosis</h4>\n<p>A very rare <b>single-gene</b> disorder (inherited or new) that grows <b>benign tumors throughout the body</b>. What you see depends on <b>where the tumors are</b>: <b>brain</b> (cognitive disability, <b>seizures</b>, ADHD; seizure drugs can cause <b>gingival overgrowth</b>), <b>kidney</b> (impaired function, affecting how you dose local anesthetic), <b>skin</b> (light-colored patches; facial growths resembling acne), and the <b>mouth</b> (weakened enamel if tumors form while teeth develop, and <b>gingival fibromas</b> or overgrowth).</p>\n<h4>Apert syndrome</h4>\n<p>A genetic disorder of skull development, almost always a <b>new mutation</b>, about <b>1 in 65,000</b> births. The skull and facial bones fuse too early (<b>craniosynostosis</b>), giving <b>dolichocephaly</b>, <b>hypertelorism</b> and <b>midface deficiency</b>. Also <b>syndactyly</b> (webbed or mitten-like hands and feet), <b>obstructive sleep apnea</b> (midface-deficient patients struggle with the airway), ear and sinus infections, and hearing loss. Oral features: <b>severe crowding</b>, a <b>highly arched palate</b>, an <b>anterior open bite</b> (diverging planes), midface hypoplasia with <b>Class III</b> malocclusion (a deficient maxilla, not a big mandible), and <b>delayed eruption</b>. Orthodontics is often the hardest part.</p>\n<h4>Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</h4>\n<p>A mutation of the <b>HPRT1</b> gene causes <b>overproduction of uric acid</b>, with neurologic and behavioral abnormalities: involuntary movements, usually <b>unable to walk</b>, profound ID, and above all <b>self-injury</b>: head banging (protective helmets) and <b>self-biting</b>, including biting off <b>lips and fingers</b>. Almost exclusively <b>males</b>.</p>\n<p>Dr. Mitchell's most important point: it may be necessary to do <b>full-mouth extractions</b> to stop the self-mutilation. Some dentists call it overtreatment, but case reports show that failing to extract early led to severe disfigurement. Oral appliances to stop the biting are hard to keep up for life, and the condition never goes away. It's a careful ethical decision with the family, but removing the teeth is not doing harm: it may be the best treatment.</p>\n<h4>Pierre Robin sequence</h4>\n<p><b>Severe micrognathia/retrognathia</b> (a hypoplastic, backward-rotated mandible), <b>glossoptosis</b>, and a <b>U-shaped cleft palate</b> (the tongue impacted in the palate as it forms). The result is severe <b>respiratory difficulty</b>; the tongue can cause <b>asphyxia</b>, and many have a <b>tracheotomy</b>. Cognitive function is usually <b>normal</b>. <b>Airway management</b> is the big issue: severe cases must be treated <b>in the OR, intubated</b>; orthognathic surgery can improve the face and airway. Routine cleanings can be done in the chair with care, and a tracheotomy, kept clear, actually makes treatment simpler.</p>\n<h4>Fetal alcohol syndrome</h4>\n<p>Environmental, not genetic: too much alcohol in pregnancy. General features: small head, below-average height and weight, developmental delay, heart and kidney problems, deformed limbs or fingers, poor coordination, vision problems. Face: low nasal bridge, epicanthal folds, small eye openings, short nose, flat midface, <b>smooth philtrum</b>, <b>thin upper lip</b>, mild <b>micrognathia</b> (hypoplastic mandible), and cleft palate in some. Behavior: hyperactivity, lack of focus, poor judgment, learning disabilities and mood swings, but usually <b>mild to moderate</b>, so many live in society, some never diagnosed. A \"difficult patient\" with these features may have undiagnosed FAS.</p>\n<h4>Cornelia de Lange syndrome</h4>\n<p><b>Slow growth before and after birth</b>, <b>short stature</b>, <b>moderate to severe ID</b> (more profound than most here), and abnormal bones in the arms, hands and fingers. Face: <b>thick eyebrows</b> (nearly a unibrow), a short nose with an upturned tip, a long smooth philtrum, a thin upper lip and downturned corners. Oral: micrognathia, <b>cleft palate in 20%</b>, <b>delayed eruption</b>, <b>widely spaced teeth</b> with <b>microdontia</b>. They often present <b>severe behavior problems</b> (combative), so they more often end up in institutional or sedation settings.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Syndrome</th><th>Cause</th><th>Intellect</th><th>Dental points</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Fragile X</td><td>FMR1 repeats on the X chromosome; mostly boys</td><td>Mental delay; autistic behaviors</td><td>Long face, prominent jaw</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prader-Willi</td><td>Chromosome 15q: paternal genes deleted (70%); usually new mutation</td><td>Mild to moderate</td><td>Obesity (sedation), outbursts, insensitive to pain, thick saliva: calculus, caries, gingivitis; high palate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tuberous sclerosis</td><td>Single gene; benign tumors anywhere</td><td>Depends on where the tumors are</td><td>Seizure-drug gingival overgrowth, weak enamel, gingival fibromas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apert</td><td>Craniosynostosis; almost always new; 1 in 65,000</td><td></td><td>Midface deficiency, Class III, crowding, high palate, open bite, delayed eruption; syndactyly; airway</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lesch-Nyhan</td><td>HPRT1 mutation: too much uric acid; almost only males</td><td>Profound</td><td>Self-biting of lips and fingers: may need full-mouth extractions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pierre Robin sequence</td><td>Micrognathia, glossoptosis, U-shaped cleft palate</td><td>Usually normal</td><td>Airway: severe cases treated in the OR intubated</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fetal alcohol syndrome</td><td>Alcohol in pregnancy (environmental)</td><td>Mild to moderate</td><td>Smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, small jaw, sometimes cleft</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cornelia de Lange</td><td>Slow growth before and after birth</td><td>Moderate to severe</td><td>Thick eyebrows, cleft 20%, delayed eruption, spaced teeth, microdontia; difficult behavior</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n","autism":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>CDC definition, prevalence (1 in 68 in 2012, 1 in 36 in 2020), boys vs girls, causes and what is not a cause (vaccines)</li><li>The TED talk: twin and sibling concordance, single-gene cases, number of genes, eye tracking</li><li>Characteristics and early signs</li><li>Stimming and how to respond; meltdown vs tantrum</li><li>Sensory defensiveness and ways to reduce it</li><li>Dental conditions in autism</li><li>Preparing for the visit: practice kits, scheduling, the operatory</li><li>Communication rules and the successful appointment</li><li>Advanced treatment options and the duty to refer</li></ul></div>\n<p>The autism unit is an online module of short readings and videos, largely drawn from a Crest learning module, with Dr. Mitchell's notes (\"VERY helpful and practical suggestions that I use regularly\").</p>\n<h4>What is autism?</h4>\n<p>The <b>CDC</b> defines autism spectrum disorders (ASDs) as \"a group of <b>developmental disabilities</b> that can cause significant <b>social, communication and behavioral</b> challenges. People with ASDs handle information in their brain differently than other people.\" Autism Speaks: a broad range of conditions with challenges in social skills, <b>repetitive behaviors</b>, speech and nonverbal communication.</p>\n<p><b>How common</b>: CDC <b>2012</b>, about <b>1 in 68</b> children; CDC <b>2020</b>, <b>1 in 36 (2.8%)</b>, almost double in 8 years, only partly explained by better diagnosis and awareness. Autism is <b>four times more likely in boys</b> (about 1 in 23 boys and 1 in 88 girls). It is more common than pediatric cancer, diabetes and AIDS combined.</p>\n<p><b>Causes</b> are not known. Autistic brains process sensory input in different areas, which may profoundly affect how the person experiences the world. There is a heavy <b>genetic</b> component.</p>\n<h4>The TED talk (Dr. Chung): what to know</h4>\n<ul><li><b>A spectrum</b>: Justin, 13, nonverbal, communicates with an iPad and bangs his head when upset; Gabriel, 13, gifted at math but struggles with conversation and eye contact. Same diagnosis.</li><li><b>Prevalence</b>: then 1 in 88; part of the rise is labeling and resources (legislation in the late 1980s and early 1990s), more awareness, and a <b>wider definition</b>.</li><li><b>Vaccines do not cause autism.</b> The original study was <b>fraudulent</b> and retracted by the <i>Lancet</i>, and its author lost his license. The IOM and CDC found no credible evidence. Thimerosal was removed from vaccines (the talk says 1992) with no effect on prevalence.</li><li><b>Associations</b>: advanced <b>paternal</b> age; exposures during pregnancy such as <b>valproic acid</b> (taken for epilepsy) and some infections.</li><li><b>Males 4 to 1</b>, for reasons not understood.</li><li><b>Concordance</b>: <b>identical twins 77%</b> (not 100%: genes aren't everything), <b>fraternal twins 31%</b>, and plain <b>siblings</b> lower still, which suggests shared exposures in the womb matter too. Genetics plays a bigger role in autism than in cancer, heart disease or diabetes.</li><li><b>Single-gene cases</b>: a <b>new (de novo)</b> mutation, not inherited, can cause autism without a family history. The Simons Foundation study of 2,600 families found a single powerful genetic factor in about <b>25%</b>; 75% are still unexplained. An estimated <b>200-400 genes</b> can cause autism, and they fit into pathways.</li><li><b>Early diagnosis</b>: Ami Klin's <b>eye tracking</b> shows infants who will develop autism look at the mouth, nose or elsewhere instead of the <b>eyes</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Characteristics</h4>\n<p>ASD begins <b>before age 3</b> and lasts for life, though symptoms improve. Some show hints in the first months; some seem typical until <b>18-24 months</b> and then stop gaining skills or lose them. A person with ASD might:</p>\n<ul><li>Not respond to their name by <b>12 months</b>; not point at objects of interest by <b>14 months</b>; not play pretend by <b>18 months</b></li><li>Avoid eye contact or want to be alone; have trouble understanding others' feelings or talking about their own</li><li>Have delayed speech; repeat words or phrases (<b>echolalia</b>)</li><li>Get upset by minor changes; have obsessive interests</li><li>Flap their hands, rock or spin</li><li>React unusually to sounds, smells, tastes, sights or touch; have <b>no real sense of danger</b></li><li>Seem not to hear people but respond to other sounds; not look where someone points</li></ul>\n<p>Ami Klin's work: infants on the spectrum don't watch the \"socially interactive\" parts of a scene (faces, speaking), so their brains fail to learn the usual social cues. They may be brilliant, average or profoundly disabled, but they share <b>difficulty interacting socially</b>. (His video is supplemental, not required.)</p>\n<h4>Stimming</h4>\n<p><b>Self-stimulatory behavior</b> (\"stimming,\" \"stereotypy\"): repetitive hand flapping, rocking, repeating phrases or sounds, moving things in front of the eyes. It happens when the person is bored, overwhelmed, nervous, or happy. <b>Before treatment, ask</b> whether the patient stims, what it looks like and how to respond. If it isn't known and you see it: <b>unless it disrupts dental work, don't feel you must stop or change it</b>. If it does, <b>redirect</b>: show another item or give the hands something else to do.</p>\n<h4>Meltdowns</h4>\n<p>An autistic child is <b>not</b> throwing a temper tantrum. A <b>tantrum</b> seeks attention (the child checks for a reaction), avoids self-injury, tries to manipulate, and ends as soon as the goal is reached. In a <b>meltdown</b>, the child is struggling to cope: doesn't look or care who reacts, doesn't consider their own safety, the meltdown runs on its own power and tapers off slowly, <b>no one feels in control</b>, and past a certain point nothing satisfies until it is over.</p>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Tantrum (typical child)</th><th>Meltdown (autism)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Wants attention; checks whether anyone is reacting</td><td>Doesn't look or care whether anyone reacts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Avoids getting hurt</td><td>Doesn't consider their own safety</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tries to manipulate the situation</td><td>Struggling to cope with the environment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stops as soon as the goal is reached</td><td>Runs on its own power and tapers off slowly; nothing satisfies until it's over</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td>No one feels in control</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>It's the clinician's responsibility to find out beforehand whether the patient has meltdowns, <b>what triggers them</b> and <b>what warning signs</b> they give. And show compassion: the parent may feel strife, grief, even embarrassment, and the patient may have no other way to communicate fear and frustration.</p>\n<h4>Sensory defensiveness</h4>\n<p>Autism and <b>sensory defensiveness</b> are not the same, but patients often have both. It may relate to <b>sensory integration disorders</b>: the brain misprocesses sensation so it is uncomfortable or painful (a shirt tag feels like a cut; a noise hurts). Sensory defensiveness is the <b>defensive reaction</b> to protect against it. In the dental visit:</p>\n<ul><li>Pulling away from or overreacting to unexpected touch, especially to the face</li><li>Oversensitivity to the hygienist's cleaning</li><li>Fear of moving backward in the chair; the bright overhead light; equipment noises (even the polishing brush); unexpected office noises (intercoms, alarms, beeps)</li><li>Extreme dislike of the <b>texture</b> of prophy paste</li><li>Overreactive <b>gag</b> to instruments, x-ray materials, or the smell or feel of gloves</li></ul>\n<p>If fear isn't respected it escalates to \"<b>flight or fight</b>\": first trying to escape, then becoming more and more physical. A child may tolerate one stimulus but not several at once.</p>\n<p><b>Reducing sensitivity</b>: the <b>x-ray vest</b> worn all appointment (weight and <b>deep pressure</b>); <b>sunglasses</b>; <b>warn before each step</b>; calming music on headphones; <b>firm touch</b>; oral deep pressure or vibration at home before the visit (electric toothbrush, mini massager, Toothette); something very <b>chewy</b> before the visit; a fidget toy that gives \"heavy work\"; a heavy or tight hat.</p>\n<h4>Dental conditions in autism</h4>\n<p>Generally the same oral health issues as everyone, and <b>no physical differences</b> such as missing or extra teeth or malocclusion (unlike Down syndrome or CP). But several are more common:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Caries: higher</b>. Fixed diets (one Sparks patient drinks only one brand of juice) and oral sensory defensiveness that prevents regular cleaning.</li><li><b>Attrition: higher</b>. Grinding to relieve anxiety, or as stimming.</li><li><b>Erosion: higher</b>, from more <b>GERD</b>.</li><li><b>Periodontitis: slightly higher</b>; treatment is hard because of behavior and because many are on <b>Medicaid</b>, which has limited or no adult dental benefit.</li><li><b>Trauma: higher</b>. A reduced sense of danger, damaging habits, and a higher incidence of <b>abuse</b>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Incidence</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Caries</td><td>Higher</td><td>Fixed diets (one patient drinks one brand of juice), cleaning not tolerated</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Attrition</td><td>Higher</td><td>Grinding for anxiety relief or self-stimulation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Erosion</td><td>Higher</td><td>More GERD</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Periodontitis</td><td>Slightly higher</td><td>Behavior; Medicaid's limited adult dental benefit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trauma</td><td>Higher</td><td>Reduced sense of danger, habits, more abuse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tooth number, malocclusion</td><td>No difference</td><td>Unlike Down syndrome or CP</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Preparing for the appointment</h4>\n<p><b>Practice kits</b>: a few weeks before, the caregiver picks up a kit and learns to use it, practicing several times a week, enjoyably, never forced, staying neutral and positive. It might hold a prophy angle, saliva ejector, fluoride varnish brush, bib and holder, gloves and mask for the caregiver, a <b>disposable XCP holder without the metal bar</b>, a cotton-tip applicator, a disposable mirror, dry angles, and pictures of each item in the mouth. Practice lying down to have teeth brushed, with a <b>dry toothbrush</b> so the patient won't choke.</p>\n<p><b>Scheduling</b>: <b>mornings</b> beat afternoons (a day of school and therapy is taxing; trust the parent if they say otherwise). The <b>first appointment of the day</b> is ideal: minimal waiting, a quiet office and fresh staff, sterilizers and drills silent, an empty waiting room, and cleaning smells settled overnight. If possible start <b>ten minutes before</b> regular scheduling, straight to the operatory, which also gives a longer appointment.</p>\n<p><b>Be ready</b>: everything you might need out on the counter within arm's reach (walking away and opening drawers distracts and adds noise). <b>No perfume or scented lotion.</b></p>\n<p><b>The office and operatory</b>: overhead music off; <b>sterilizers and the ultrasonic off</b>; lights low (people with ASD are particularly sensitive to <b>fluorescent</b> light: use the chair light or loupe light); <b>always offer sunglasses</b>; noise-cancelling headphones (ask the family to bring theirs); phone ringers down; wait in a consultation room instead of the busy waiting room; no flashing screen savers on visible monitors.</p>\n<h4>Communication (Dr. Mitchell uses these regularly)</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Short, concise statements; nothing abstract.</b> \"Open your mouth,\" not \"open like an alligator\": people with ASD understand language <b>literally</b>, and a patient who loves alligators may never come back from the topic.</li><li><b>\"Hands on belly\"</b>: friendlier than \"put your hands down\" or \"stop,\" and it works wonders.</li><li><b>Give the instruction once and wait patiently.</b> Each repetition has to be processed again; the wait time is longer. \"Open your mouth,\" then \"like this,\" and show them.</li><li><b>Keep language consistent.</b> Pick one phrase, have every staff member use it, and document what works.</li><li><b>How you say it</b>: a sing-song, high-pitched voice may not register as an instruction. Use a <b>direct, even, neutral tone and a deeper voice</b> (not loud or rude).</li><li><b>Excitement can be scary</b>: clapping and yelling \"You did it!\" can keep a patient from ever opening again. Praise freely, but <b>calmly</b>.</li><li><b>Don't ask questions that allow \"no\"</b> when no isn't an option (\"Can I polish your teeth now?\"). Say \"It is time to polish your teeth. Please open your mouth.\" Consent was obtained beforehand; treatment decisions are made in advance, not during.</li><li><b>Use visual supports</b>: pictures are concrete, lower anxiety and improve understanding.</li></ul>\n<h4>The successful appointment</h4>\n<p><b>Introduction</b>: spend a few minutes just talking and being in the same space. Don't be offended by no handshake, high five or eye contact. Be yourself, as with any child.</p>\n<p><b>In the operatory</b>: brief small talk (the history came from the parent interview); let them sit a few minutes; use a <b>visual schedule</b> and stick to its order, removing each picture as its task is done; be flexible: offer breaks, adapt, or stop.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Start lying down</b>: if the chair moving back bothers them, have them get up, position the chair, have them lie down, and have them <b>sit up on their own</b> before you raise it.</li><li><b>Start at the midline</b> (central incisors) with the mouth slightly closed, so they get used to the vibration before you reach gag-triggering areas; they taste the paste or fluoride only at the end. Always go from <b>least to most invasive</b>.</li><li><b>Count to 10</b>: the clinician controls when the break comes, the patient only works briefly, and it distracts. If you stop whenever they raise a hand or make a noise, treatment never ends. Count fast when they struggle, slow when they cooperate, and always reach 10 so \"ten\" means a break (use a smaller number if needed, consistently).</li><li><b>Reward and praise</b>: calm praise; rewards such as a minute of their video after the upper teeth are cleaned.</li></ul>\n<h4>Advanced treatment options</h4>\n<p>Many autistic patients are high functioning and can be treated in a typical office. Some have profound behavioral challenges that endanger them and the staff. <b>Never attempt treatment you can't do safely</b> (\"first, do no harm\"), but the <b>ADA Code of Ethics</b> says don't simply decline: <b>refer</b>.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Oral conscious sedation</b> (an Alabama oral sedation license; lorazepam, diazepam): only <b>mild</b> sedation, of limited value, though families hope it will \"knock them out.\"</li><li><b>IV sedation</b>: a higher license; the Alabama Board inspects the office and requires training <b>even if an anesthesiologist is brought in</b>. Better behavior control but more risk, and many won't tolerate the IV start.</li><li><b>General anesthesia</b>: lets many be treated who otherwise couldn't, but <b>OR access is very limited</b>. Under 21, pediatric dentists usually have access; very few Alabama hospitals open ORs to adults.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What they offer</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>UAB Hospital Dentistry (GPR)</td><td>Medically complex patients; some comprehensive adult care in UAB's ORs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Oral surgeons</td><td>OR or IV sedation, but extractions only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The Foundry Dental Clinic (GPR, Bessemer)</td><td>An anesthesiologist comes to the clinic; comprehensive adult care</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sparks Dental Clinic</td><td>Dental home for complex patients; Children's OR under 21; oral sedation and protective stabilization for older patients</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pediatric dentists</td><td>OR or in-office sedation for children; a few accept adults</td></tr>\n<tr><td>General dentists (often AEGD or GPR trained)</td><td>Some accept special needs patients; one or two have hospital privileges</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n","prevention":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Fluoride varnish: why Sparks uses it exclusively, and the ADA's non-restorative guideline numbers</li><li>Restorative choices that prevent caries: SDF, RMGI, stainless steel crowns</li><li>Calculus: hidden subgingival rings, sickle vs curette, labial calculus on lower incisors, g-tube patients</li><li>Rubber cup technique and pulsing for the gag reflex; recall intervals</li><li>Home care: modified brushes, flossers, positions for brushing</li><li>Home fluorides and the 8-year rule; contact time</li><li>Diet: sugar-sweetened beverages, the key question, better drinks, artificial sweeteners and the 10-calorie rule</li></ul></div>\n<p>This lecture looks at prevention from the special needs point of view, in two parts: <b>in-office</b> prevention (caries, then periodontal disease) and <b>at-home</b> prevention (hygiene, medicaments and, maybe most important, diet).</p>\n<h4>Fluoride varnish in the office</h4>\n<p>Sparks uses <b>fluoride varnish exclusively</b>: no gels or foams in trays. Most patients couldn't tolerate a tray for the <b>four minutes</b> a foam or gel needs (brushing a gel on has no real evidence). Varnish causes <b>fewer gag reflex problems</b>, is a <b>stronger</b> fluoride applied right where you want it, is applied in seconds with a brush (saliva helps it set), and stays on, a reservoir of fluoride. And there's <b>no waiting</b>: the patient can eat and drink right away (gels and foams: no eating for 20-30 minutes).</p>\n<p>Varnish doesn't only prevent new lesions; it stops existing ones growing. The <b>ADA's 2018 clinical guideline on non-restorative treatment of carious lesions</b>:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Non-cavitated occlusal</b> lesions (the most common caries): <b>sealant over the lesion plus fluoride varnish every 3-6 months</b>, strong evidence, <b>67 of 100 arrested</b>. Varnish alone: about 40-something of 100.</li><li><b>Non-cavitated approximal</b> (shadows on bitewings): <b>5% fluoride varnish every 3-6 months</b>, <b>twice the chance</b> of reversing or stopping progression, <b>46 of 100</b>; simple and cost-effective.</li><li><b>Non-cavitated facial or lingual</b> (class V): varnish or gel.</li><li><b>Root caries</b>: <b>Prevident 5000</b> used at home.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Lesion</th><th>Best option</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Non-cavitated occlusal</td><td>Sealant plus 5% fluoride varnish every 3-6 months</td><td>67 of 100 arrested (varnish alone: in the 40s)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-cavitated approximal</td><td>5% fluoride varnish every 3-6 months</td><td>2 times the chance of arrest or reversal: 46 of 100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-cavitated facial or lingual</td><td>Fluoride varnish (or gel)</td><td>Effective</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Root caries</td><td>Prevident 5000 at home</td><td>Effective</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Materials that prevent caries</h4>\n<p>When cavitation occurs, pick materials that prevent future decay: <b>silver diamine fluoride</b> (arrests small lesions, turns them black; good when behavior makes restoring hard), <b>resin-modified glass ionomers</b> (Fuji II LC, Fuji IX, Ketac Nano; they <b>release fluoride</b>, for small and moderate cavities in patients who can't tolerate traditional restorations), and <b>stainless steel crowns</b> (for larger lesions, on permanent teeth too, protecting the whole tooth).</p>\n<h4>Periodontal disease: calculus control</h4>\n<p>Periodontal disease is the most common problem in this population, and calculus control is the biggest issue. A mouth may show <b>no visible calculus</b> but <b>swollen gingiva</b>: almost always there is calculus under the margin, often a <b>ring just below the tissue</b>. Explore under the margins. (Twice in one week at Sparks, patients charted as having no calculus had extensive subgingival calculus, one with mobile lower incisors.) Interproximal calculus may show on bitewings; air from the syringe can reveal dark areas under the gingiva.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Supragingival</b>: a <b>sickle scaler</b>, its point into the interproximal, sliding to the tissue and lifting.</li><li><b>Subgingival</b>: a <b>curette</b> (rounded end), slid below the margin; explore with the <b>toe</b>, engage what you feel, and lift with long, smooth strokes against the tooth.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Calculus on the facial of the lower incisors</b> is a pattern here: the patient tightens the <b>lower lip</b> and blocks the brush. Teach caregivers to <b>pull the lower lip down</b> to reach the gingival margin; for a very tight lip, put a finger where the lower lip and buccal mucosa join and roll the lip out.</p>\n<h4>Rubber cup prophy</h4>\n<p>Focus on the <b>gingival margin</b>: the cup must touch the tissue (not for long periods), follow the tooth's contour, and let the spin do the work at moderate to high speed. <b>Roll into the interproximal areas.</b> For sensitive gag reflexes, work in <b>pulses</b>: 1-2 seconds on the tooth, then off, removing the stimulus before the gag fires. This matters most on the <b>lingual of the lower molars</b>, near the tongue.</p>\n<p><b>Gastrostomy-tube-fed patients</b> often have <b>very heavy calculus</b> and hypersensitive gag reflexes (one was treated sitting at 90 degrees with the head tipped forward). Remove calculus from the <b>buccal and lingual</b> but <b>leave it on the occlusal</b>: a natural sealant.</p>\n<p><b>Recall</b>: 6 months is most common but not always best: 3 or 4 months, even monthly. Most third-party payers won't pay more than twice a year, and some require 6 months between, so families pay; keep cost down by charging only for the cleaning, not an exam or fluoride each time.</p>\n<h4>Home hygiene aids</h4>\n<p>Real prevention happens at home, every day. The <b>Surround toothbrush</b> (Specialized Care Co.) has three bristle heads that clean both sides and the occlusal at once: faster, though <b>not as efficient</b> as a regular brush, so it's for patients who tolerate only a short brushing. For patients who can brush but have poor grip or can't bend their arms: <b>enlarge the handle</b> (foam, a tennis ball, a washcloth with rubber bands, acrylic, putty) or <b>lengthen it</b> with a tongue blade. A flosser whose floss sits at <b>90 degrees to the handle</b> reaches the back teeth easily, for the patient or a caregiver. <b>Electric</b> (especially <b>Sonicare</b>-type) brushes can be better, and the vibration comforts some sensory patients (not all); Dr. Mitchell is more cautious about round \"spin brush\" heads, which can be too big.</p>\n<h4>Teaching caregivers: positions</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Standing in front</b>: for more cooperative patients; little control of the hands; the <b>lowest gag risk</b> (the patient can lean forward).</li><li><b>Patient sits, caregiver stands behind</b> with the head against the caregiver's arm: more head control, good for tall patients; some more gag risk.</li><li><b>Recliner</b>, laid back, caregiver behind: replicates the dental chair, so it <b>trains</b> the patient; more gag risk.</li><li><b>Lying in bed</b>: for patients who can't stand or prefer lying; more control; <b>the most gag risk</b> (turn the head if needed).</li><li><b>Lying on the floor, two caregivers</b>: the head in caregiver 1's lap, who also holds the hands (crossed is easier to control); caregiver 2 brushes. Most control of less cooperative patients; most gag risk.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Good for</th><th>Gag risk</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Standing, facing the patient</td><td>Cooperative patients; little control of arms</td><td>Lowest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Patient sits, caregiver stands behind</td><td>Tall patients; controls the head</td><td>Some increase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recliner, caregiver behind</td><td>Trains for the dental chair</td><td>More</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lying in bed</td><td>Patients who can't stand or prefer lying; more control</td><td>Most</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lying on the floor, two caregivers</td><td>Less cooperative patients: one holds head and hands, one brushes</td><td>Most</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Preventive medicaments</h4>\n<p><b>Don't prescribe fluoride rinses or gels until at least age 8</b>: they will likely swallow it, and with teeth still developing, risk <b>fluorosis</b>. Custom trays are rarely tolerated, and few can rinse and spit, so products are usually <b>brushed on</b>. Consider <b>contact time</b>: a product that needs 30-60 seconds does nothing for a patient who tolerates 5-10.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Prevident 5000</b>: a high-fluoride (<b>sodium fluoride</b>) toothpaste, used instead of their regular paste. Dr. Mitchell: brush it on at night, spit or wipe out the excess, and <b>go to bed without rinsing</b>.</li><li><b>GelKam</b>: <b>stannous fluoride</b>, fluoride only, <b>not a toothpaste</b>: a second fluoride for a patient who likes their own paste.</li><li><b>MI Paste Plus</b>: calcium and phosphate <b>plus sodium fluoride</b>. Plain MI Paste has no fluoride and weak evidence; use the Plus.</li><li><b>Rinses</b> (ACT, fluoride Listerine) if they can rinse. A 6'5\" autistic patient used Listerine but refused toothpaste: brush without paste and switch to a fluoride Listerine. If they can't rinse, apply with a <b>Toothette</b> (a sponge on a stick), repeatedly for <b>30-60 seconds</b>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>What it is</th><th>How to use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Prevident 5000</td><td>High-fluoride toothpaste (sodium fluoride)</td><td>Brush on at night, spit or wipe out the excess, go to bed without rinsing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GelKam</td><td>Fluoride only (stannous fluoride), not a toothpaste</td><td>Same way, alongside a toothpaste they like</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MI Paste Plus</td><td>Calcium and phosphate plus sodium fluoride</td><td>Same way (plain MI Paste has no fluoride)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fluoride rinses (ACT, fluoride Listerine)</td><td>Mouthwash</td><td>Only if they can rinse; otherwise a Toothette for 30-60 seconds</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Diet: maybe the most important</h4>\n<p>For many families good oral hygiene isn't realistic; what they can control is the sugar available. <b>Sugar-sweetened beverages (SSBs)</b> are the biggest problem: they <b>bathe every tooth</b> and are sipped <b>over long periods</b> (a woman in a wheelchair who sipped Coke all day through a straw needed extensive treatment despite a terrible gag reflex). The diagnostic question: <b>\"In a typical day, what does he or she drink the most?\"</b></p>\n<p>Families may resist: the drink is a <b>treat</b> for someone who can't do so much else, taking it away can cause a meltdown, and one family told Dr. Mitchell to take all the teeth out rather than give up Dr Pepper.</p>\n<p><b>Healthy drinks</b>: <b>water</b>, <b>plain milk</b> (some studies suggest it even prevents caries, but not chocolate or strawberry), and <b>unsweet tea</b> (stain is removable). <b>100% juice is not on the list</b>: it's high in natural sugar (a glass of apple juice is like eating five to ten apples).</p>\n<p><b>Artificially sweetened</b> drinks (Crystal Light and store brands, diet and \"Zero\" sodas with some erosion risk, Splenda and Stevia in tea) keep the treat without the caries. Teach families to read the label: <b>10 calories or fewer per serving</b> is not a caries risk (Coke 110, Coke Zero 0, Dr Pepper Ten 10).</p>\n<p><b>Are artificial sweeteners bad?</b> There's controversy, especially in the autism community, where some families are committed to \"all natural\" diets, though nothing about soft drinks is natural, and almost all SSBs use <b>high-fructose corn syrup</b>. For aspartame the FDA's acceptable daily intake is <b>50 mg/kg/day</b>: about <b>19 cans (6.7 liters) of Coke Zero a day</b> for a 150-lb adult.</p>\n<p>Dr. Mitchell's recommendations: talk with the <b>primary care provider</b> about sweeteners; day to day, use artificially sweetened drinks instead of SSBs when the teeth can't be cleaned well, <b>limited to 2-3 a day</b>; on special occasions, have fun (Halloween is fine; two weeks of candy afterward is the problem).</p>\n<h4>Why it matters</h4>\n<p>Prevention is the most important thing we do for this population, because treating disease can be close to impossible. Many patients can't say they hurt: chronic inflammation hurts, and pain can make them combative (an autistic patient was hitting his face because his mouth hurt). COVID's shutdown brought more patients in pain with worse behavior.</p>\n","mental":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Mental illness vs intellectual disability (mental retardation in the reading)</li><li>Major depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, dementia, and alcohol and substance abuse: what each is like and its dental implications</li><li>Medication side effects (xerostomia rates, extrapyramidal symptoms, tardive dyskinesia) and drug interactions with dental drugs</li><li>Managing behavior and prescribing safely</li></ul></div>\n<p>Dr. Mitchell doesn't consider himself an expert in mental health, which is more of an adult dentistry issue, so instead of a lecture he posted an edited reading: the Southern Association of Institutional Dentists self-study course, <b>Module 9, \"Clinical concerns in dental care for persons with mental illness.\"</b> He asked you to understand each illness and its characteristics, but to <b>focus on the dental issues</b> and how you would handle them in practice. (The reading is old: it uses the DSM-IV and the term \"mental retardation.\")</p>\n<h4>Mental illness vs intellectual disability</h4>\n<p><b>Mental illness</b> is the inability to perform major activities of daily living because of a <b>psychiatric or emotional disorder</b>; <b>intellectual disability</b> is a similar dysfunction from a <b>cognitive deficit</b>. They can coexist (dual diagnosis). About <b>3%</b> of the population has intellectual disability, while about <b>1 in 6 (17%)</b> has a diagnosable mental disorder. ID is <b>permanent</b>; mental illness may be <b>sporadic</b> (depression once or twice in a life). Psychotropic medications let most people with mental illness live in the community; institutions now mainly serve chronic, refractory cases and acute exacerbations.</p>\n<h4>Major depression</h4>\n<p>An <b>affective disorder</b> with a prolonged depressed mood: loss of interest or pleasure, appetite and weight loss, insomnia, low energy, feelings of worthlessness, poor concentration, thoughts of death. About <b>6%</b> have severe depression; it is a factor in more than 30,000 U.S. suicides a year. In the depths of depression there is an almost total absence of oral hygiene. Common dental findings: <b>poor oral hygiene, rampant decay, advanced generalized periodontal disease, multiple missing teeth, ill-fitting prostheses, orofacial pain syndromes, xerostomia</b>, and poor nutrition and diet.</p>\n<p>Treatment: medication (SSRIs, tricyclics, MAO inhibitors), psychotherapy, diet, exercise, sleep, occasionally <b>electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)</b>. Dental care addresses hygiene, decay and perio (motivating the patient is hard) and <b>xerostomia</b>: seen in <b>14% on Prozac</b> and <b>45% on tricyclics</b>. Patients often fight dry mouth with cariogenic drinks, candy and gum; prescribe <b>saliva substitutes</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Drug interactions</b>: epinephrine may be used <b>with prudence</b> with tricyclics, but <b>levonordefrin (Neo-Cobefrin)</b> and <b>norepinephrine (Levophed)</b> are <b>contraindicated</b> with tricyclics. The reading says epinephrine is contraindicated with <b>MAO inhibitors</b>, and <b>meperidine (Demerol) is absolutely contraindicated</b> with them. Use sedatives judiciously with tricyclics.</p>\n<p>For <b>ECT</b>, the dentist may be asked to make a <b>mouth guard</b>, and should address loose teeth, gross calculus and loose prostheses beforehand (aspiration risk) and document existing conditions (liability).</p>\n<h4>Bipolar disorder</h4>\n<p>Manic-depressive disorder: alternating, prolonged episodes of extreme elation and depression; about <b>1%</b> of people, strongly familial. <b>Mania</b>: hyperactivity, pressured loud rapid speech, distractibility, grandiose delusions, days without sleep, rapid shifts to anger. Episodes last about 3 months and may recur every 3-9 years; depressive episodes outnumber manic ones. Smoking, alcohol and cocaine use are common.</p>\n<p>Dental findings in <b>mania</b>: <b>abraded oral mucosa and cervical tooth abrasion</b> from <b>over-vigorous brushing or flossing</b>. In the depressive phase: the same as major depression. Treated with anti-seizure drugs, neuroleptics and <b>lithium</b> (effective in 70% within 3 weeks): <b>stomatitis</b> and <b>xerostomia</b> (73% in one study). <b>NSAIDs may decrease lithium's renal clearance</b> and allow toxic levels (short-term use may be fine); use <b>benzodiazepines with caution</b> (CNS depression).</p>\n<h4>Schizophrenia</h4>\n<p>A psychotic disorder with personality disorganization lasting at least 6 months; about <b>1-2%</b> will be hospitalized for it. Bizarre <b>delusions</b> (persecutory: being spied on; of reference: a TV show is about them), <b>hallucinations</b> (insulting voices), incoherent speech, <b>flat affect</b>, catatonia or bizarre mannerisms. Confused, depressed, withdrawn or anxious patients neglect or refuse dental care; with <b>xerostomia</b> from antipsychotics, that means more decay and periodontal disease.</p>\n<p>Approach <b>paranoid</b> patients <b>slowly and non-threateningly</b>, with no sudden movements; warn them what to expect and show them each next step.</p>\n<p>Antipsychotics (neuroleptics, phenothiazines) cause short-term <b>extrapyramidal symptoms</b> (agitation, neck spasms or torticollis, oculogyric crises; treated with IM Benadryl) and long-term <b>tardive dyskinesia</b>: trismus, swallowing problems, <b>tongue protrusion</b>, constant <b>lip and jaw movements</b> (lip chewing, tongue wiping, smacking); controlled with Cogentin and Artane. <b>Akathisia</b>: restlessness, can't sit still, wants to get up during the appointment. Dental <b>sedatives</b> can combine with neuroleptics to cause excessive <b>respiratory depression</b>; local anesthetic with epinephrine is fine in normotensive patients.</p>\n<h4>Dementia</h4>\n<p>A loss of intellectual function severe enough to interfere with social or occupational life (memory, judgment, abstract thought), including <b>Alzheimer's</b>. About 2-3% at 65-79 and <b>over 20% at 80</b> and older. Poor short-term memory brings agitation and disorientation in unfamiliar settings; advanced disease brings incontinence, loss of self-care, contractures and <b>apraxia</b> (unable to use objects such as a toothbrush). Dental problems: <b>maxillofacial injuries from falls</b>, traumatic ulcers, poor hygiene, <b>extensive coronal and root caries</b>, periodontal disease, missing or broken teeth, attrition, salivary dysfunction, ridge atrophy, nonfunctional prostheses, <b>xerostomia</b> and <b>candidiasis</b> (5-20% on antipsychotics).</p>\n<p><b>Complete dental treatment as early as possible</b> in progressive dementia, because cooperation declines; take <b>full-mouth radiographs</b> early for future reference. Consult the treatment team. Chlorhexidine and denture scrubs help, but many can't rinse for 30 seconds and swallow everything; more frequent prophylaxis and fluoride may be the only option for root caries. Prescribe cautiously: in the elderly, a single dose of most drugs gives about <b>twice the peak blood level and twice the half-life</b>. The communication techniques used for ID work here too: reassurance, nonverbal communication, repetition, no abstract terms, <b>nouns rather than pronouns</b>, short words and sentences.</p>\n<h4>Alcohol and other substance abuse</h4>\n<p>Alcoholism is estimated at <b>28-50%</b> in psychiatric hospitals. Dental implications: poor hygiene, neglect, <b>attrition (bruxism)</b>, <b>xerostomia</b>, <b>oral cancer</b> (with heavy smoking: screen carefully), <b>candidiasis</b>, <b>impaired healing</b> and <b>bleeding</b> (liver damage), and orofacial trauma (osteomyelitis can follow compound mandible fractures).</p>\n<ul><li>Before oral surgery, a <b>bleeding profile</b> (CBC, PT, PTT, bleeding time) is often indicated; more antibiotics after surgery may be needed.</li><li>Tolerance to alcohol brings <b>tolerance to sedatives</b>: higher doses may be needed. Drugs metabolized by the liver need caution (lidocaine, mepivacaine, ampicillin, aspirin, acetaminophen, codeine, diazepam, barbiturates); an <b>ester</b> local has been recommended in cirrhosis; long-time alcoholics may need much more local anesthetic.</li><li>IV drug users carry higher <b>HBV and HIV</b> risk.</li><li><b>Cocaine</b> causes cardiac irritability: <b>epinephrine is strongly contraindicated within 48 hours of cocaine use</b>.</li><li><b>Recovering</b> patients: avoid mood-altering drugs, including <b>nitrous oxide</b> and nearly all analgesics except <b>NSAIDs</b> (aspirin, ibuprofen, diflunisal). If narcotics are truly needed (major surgery), prescribe only after NSAIDs fail, enough for about <b>48 hours</b>, dispensed by someone else if possible, never for merely anticipated pain; a long-acting local such as <b>bupivacaine</b> reduces the need.</li><li>Patients on <b>Antabuse (disulfiram)</b> must avoid alcohol-based mouthwashes and products.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"rv-table-wrap\"><table class=\"rv-table\"><thead><tr><th>Patient takes</th><th>Caution</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Tricyclic antidepressants</td><td>Epinephrine with prudence; levonordefrin (Neo-Cobefrin) and norepinephrine (Levophed) contraindicated; sedatives with care; xerostomia in 45%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MAO inhibitors</td><td>Epinephrine contraindicated (per the reading); meperidine (Demerol) absolutely contraindicated</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SSRIs (Prozac)</td><td>Xerostomia in 14%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lithium</td><td>NSAIDs can reduce its clearance (toxicity), mostly with long use; benzodiazepines with caution; xerostomia in 73%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Antipsychotics (phenothiazines)</td><td>Sedatives can deepen respiratory depression; epinephrine fine if normotensive</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cocaine within 48 hours</td><td>Local anesthetic with epinephrine strongly contraindicated</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disulfiram (Antabuse)</td><td>No alcohol-containing mouthwashes or products</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recovering addicts</td><td>Avoid mood-altering drugs, including nitrous oxide; prefer NSAIDs; a long-acting local (bupivacaine)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>(The reading's medication list also has a misprint: it puts tricyclics such as amitriptyline under the SSRI heading.)</p>\n"},"readingsPlain":{"intro":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>What intellectual disability means, and its three parts</li><li>The old words and when they changed</li><li>Person-first language</li><li>Institutions, group homes and day programs</li><li>How common disability is, especially in Alabama</li><li>Why these patients struggle to get dental care, and your duty to refer</li><li>What causes ID, including ACEs</li></ul></div>\n<h4>The big picture</h4>\n<p>Dr. Mitchell runs the Sparks Dental Clinic at UAB. Special needs covers a lot of people, but this course is mostly about people with <b>intellectual disability</b> (ID, or IDD). Many special needs patients have no named diagnosis at all.</p>\n<h4>What ID means</h4>\n<p>ID is trouble with <b>thinking</b> and with <b>adapting</b> to new places and sensations, and it has to <b>start in childhood</b>. All three parts are needed (DSM-5). Someone who was healthy until a brain injury as an adult is special needs, but does not have ID, because it did not start in childhood.</p>\n<p>It used to be decided by an IQ test: under 70. Now it takes a long evaluation by several specialists, and it looks at adapting and at language and culture too.</p>\n<h4>The words</h4>\n<p>Words for ID keep turning into insults and getting replaced. In the early 1900s doctors said <b>feeble-minded</b> (moron, imbecile, idiot, by mental age). Then <b>mental retardation</b>. <b>Rosa's Law (2010)</b> took that term out of federal documents, and the <b>DSM-5 (2013)</b> switched to intellectual disability.</p>\n<p>Say <b>\"a patient who has Down syndrome,\"</b> not \"a Down syndrome patient.\" ID is a diagnosis, not a description of who someone is. Speaking to people with respect gets respect back.</p>\n<h4>Where people live</h4>\n<p>Before the 1950s, families were told to send people with ID to big <b>institutions</b> like Bryce Hospital. Underfunded, they became places of neglect. Geraldo Rivera exposed <b>Willowbrook</b> in the 1970s. Then came <b>deinstitutionalization</b>: care in the community. Since 1978 public schools must offer special education.</p>\n<p>Today <b>most live at home</b> with family. Many adults live in <b>group homes</b> (6-8 people and a caregiver) and spend the day at <b>day programs</b> like United Ability, the Exceptional Foundation or the Arc. Only <b>1-2%</b> live in institutions like Glenwood: the most severely affected.</p>\n<h4>How common</h4>\n<p>One in five U.S. adults has some disability, and <b>one in three in Alabama</b>, the highest in the nation. For <b>severe</b> disability it's 12.6% nationally and 16.7% in Alabama; West Virginia is highest at 19.4%. Developmental disabilities in children keep rising, partly because of better diagnosis and less shame. Cerebral palsy, hearing loss and seizure disorders have gone down.</p>\n<h4>Getting dental care</h4>\n<p>The Surgeon General's reports (2000, 2002, 2005) found these patients can't get care, partly because schools didn't train dentists. In Alabama, <b>Medicaid covers dental care until age 21</b>, then the dental benefit stops. Many adults live on Social Security and pay out of pocket.</p>\n<p>Since <b>2019, CODA</b> says graduates must be able to <b>assess and manage</b> these patients. Since <b>2018, the ADA Code of Ethics</b> says you can't turn someone away because of a disability. You don't have to treat everyone, but if you can't, you must <b>refer</b> them somewhere that can.</p>\n<h4>What causes ID</h4>\n<p>In 30-40% of cases, nobody knows. Causes can be <b>genetic</b> (a whole extra chromosome, like Down syndrome, or one faulty gene, like fragile X), something before birth (fetal alcohol syndrome is about 2%), at birth (prematurity, lack of oxygen), or after birth (injury, infection, tumors).</p>\n<p><b>ACEs</b>, adverse childhood experiences, change how a child's brain develops. Stress hormones damage the hippocampus and leave the brain on high alert, so the child reads more things as danger. The more ACEs, the bigger the effect.</p>\n<h4>What helps them learn</h4>\n<p>Talk in short, clear, concrete words. Repeat things. Keep it quiet. Keep a routine. Many have speech problems, sensitive gag reflexes and quick mood swings, but many are wonderful patients.</p>\n","behavior":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Maladaptive behavior and non-contingent escape</li><li>Talking to patients: social age, start high, show, praise, clear goals</li><li>Changing the room to help</li><li>Active vs passive stabilization, and when never to use it</li><li>Mouth props and papoose boards</li><li>Consent: express vs implied, and its three parts</li><li>Competence, beneficence and advocacy</li></ul></div>\n<h4>Maladaptive behavior</h4>\n<p><b>Maladaptive behavior</b> means a patient can't adapt to the dental office. It's only some patients with IDD, and you can't predict who.</p>\n<p>These patients deserve good care, limited only by whether they'd <b>benefit</b> from it and whether they can <b>tolerate</b> it. A profoundly disabled patient may not benefit from esthetic crowns; a patient who can't keep tissue clean may not do well with tooth-colored fillings; a fragile tooth may have to come out if fixing it is too dangerous.</p>\n<h4>Talking to patients</h4>\n<ul><li>Short, clear, concrete instructions, repeated often</li><li>Tone matters even when words don't: stay calm and kind</li><li>Talk at the patient's <b>social age</b>, but <b>start at a normal level and work down</b>. Talking down to someone who understands is insulting.</li><li><b>Show</b> them: open your own mouth, let them touch the prophy angle</li><li><b>Praise</b> good behavior a lot; correct bad behavior briefly, then praise again</li><li>Say <b>what</b> you'll do, <b>what they</b> need to do, and <b>how long</b>: \"until the count of 10\"</li></ul>\n<h4>Non-contingent escape</h4>\n<p>Set a small goal (\"five seconds,\" \"three teeth\") and <b>finish it even if they fuss</b>, then give the break. If you stop because they act up, that's <b>contingent escape</b>, and they learn that acting up works. Keep the counts the same each time.</p>\n<h4>The room</h4>\n<p>Sit some patients up at 45 degrees. Spray water in <b>short bursts</b> so they can breathe. Dim the lights for light-sensitive patients, play music they like, keep fewer people in the room, and use the same room and staff each time.</p>\n<h4>Protective stabilization</h4>\n<p>Limiting a patient's movement for a short time so you can treat safely. <b>Active</b> = a <b>person</b> holds them (head holds, hand guarding, holding arms or legs). <b>Passive</b> = a <b>device</b> (mouth props, papoose board). Even a staff member holding a head counts.</p>\n<p>It's for <b>safety</b>, like a baby's car seat. <b>Never</b> as punishment, instead of behavior guidance, or for your convenience. The best kind is the <b>least restrictive</b>, the least likely to hurt, and always <b>consented</b>. Sometimes not using it when a patient is very resistive is negligent.</p>\n<p>Head holds: cradle the head against your chest with your mirror arm, or have a helper cup the head with spread fingers on the temporal and occipital bones. For kicking, Dr. Mitchell lifts the legs off the chair by the ankles. Check circulation, and ease off when they relax. Don't use hand-over-mouth techniques (HOME, HOMAR).</p>\n<h4>Mouth props and papoose boards</h4>\n<p>The <b>Molt prop</b> opens and locks like scissors. Keep it on the <b>back teeth</b>: on front teeth it can knock them loose or out. Watch the lips. Tell the caregiver about any scrapes. Without a prop, keep your fingers in the cheek fold, not between the teeth.</p>\n<p>A <b>papoose board</b> wraps the patient snugly; Sparks uses the <b>Rainbow Wrap</b> (\"the blanket\"), with straps crossed in an X over the chest. Weighted blankets calm some autistic patients but are hard to disinfect.</p>\n<h4>Consent</h4>\n<p>Any stabilization needs <b>informed consent</b>. Without it, a dentist can be charged with assault and battery or sued for malpractice. <b>Express</b> consent is spoken or written; <b>implied</b> consent comes from actions, like an adult showing up for a cleaning.</p>\n<p>Informed consent needs three things: the person can <b>understand</b>, they're <b>informed</b> (a blanket form signed at registration doesn't count), and they agree <b>freely</b>. Write it down in detail, including why you used it, what you used, how well it worked and how long.</p>\n<h4>Ethics</h4>\n<p>A <b>competent</b> patient can say no, even to good care. If the patient isn't competent, choose what's best for them (<b>beneficence</b>). And <b>advocate</b> for them, with caregivers and with the state.</p>\n","down":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>How Down syndrome happens and its three types</li><li>Mother's age, life expectancy, intellect and behavior</li><li>Face, heart, immunity, airway and neck</li><li>The mouth: perio, NUG, missing and small teeth</li><li>Treatment, including liquid antibiotic doses</li></ul></div>\n<h4>How it happens</h4>\n<p>Down syndrome is usually an <b>extra chromosome 21</b>: 47 chromosomes instead of 46. It comes from <b>nondisjunction</b>, when the 21st pair fails to split while an egg or sperm forms. Over 90% of the time it's the <b>mother's egg</b>.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Trisomy 21, 94%</b>: a whole extra 21; 47 chromosomes</li><li><b>Translocation, 5%</b>: the extra 21 is stuck onto another chromosome (usually 14); 46 chromosomes</li><li><b>Mosaicism, 1%</b>: the error happens after conception, so only some cells have 47</li></ul>\n<p>It affects about 1 in 700 births, and the risk climbs with the mother's age (1 in 2,300 at 20, 1 in 65 at 46). Life expectancy went from <b>9 years in 1929 to 55 by the 2000s</b>.</p>\n<h4>Thinking and behavior</h4>\n<p>About 70% have mild or moderate ID. Half show some decline before 50, and every autopsy shows Alzheimer's-type brain changes. Most are <b>happy, playful and loving</b>, but they scare easily and pick up on the mood in the room. The more severe the ID, the more mistrust and the chance of violent protectiveness.</p>\n<h4>The face</h4>\n<p>The key feature is <b>midface hypoplasia</b>: a flat middle face from an underdeveloped upper jaw. Also a flat, saddle-shaped nose, low-set ears, and <b>epicanthal folds</b> (almond eyes). The small nose and airway mean many <b>breathe through the mouth</b>, so don't flood them with water.</p>\n<h4>Medical issues</h4>\n<p>About <b>half have a heart defect</b>. The most common is an <b>AV canal defect</b>. Most are fixed early and need no antibiotics. <b>Tetralogy of Fallot</b> often does. Many have valve problems they don't know about.</p>\n<p>Antibiotic prophylaxis is only for: unrepaired cyanotic defects, repairs with prosthetic material in the first six months, and repairs that left a leak next to the patch.</p>\n<p>Weaker immune systems mean more colds, ear infections and pneumonia. <b>Leukemia</b> is 15-50 times more common, but now usually curable. Thyroid problems, diabetes and celiac disease are more common. Most have <b>sleep apnea</b>, many have swallowing trouble and reflux. In <b>10-20%</b> the top two neck bones are loose (<b>atlantoaxial instability</b>), so <b>don't tip the head far back</b>.</p>\n<h4>The mouth</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Class III bite</b> because the upper jaw is small; narrow palate; spaces between teeth; big, fissured tongue</li><li>Teeth come in late; <b>missing lateral incisors</b> are common; small and peg-shaped teeth; big pulp chambers (taurodontism)</li><li><b>Gum disease is the number one problem</b>: early, fast, starting as young as 6</li><li><b>NUG</b> in 45%, often without bad breath or pain</li><li>Cavities: usually fewer, but the evidence is mixed</li></ul>\n<h4>Treatment</h4>\n<p>Start prevention early and warn families about gum disease. Parents brush for them, for life. Probe from age 6. Use chlorhexidine brushed on, and antibiotics if needed (doxycycline only after age 8-10).</p>\n<p>If they can't swallow pills, prescribe liquid. For 500 mg of amoxicillin three times a day: <b>amoxicillin 250 mg/5 mL, 10 mL three times a day</b>.</p>\n<p>Dentures and bridges rarely work. As ID gets more severe, choose <b>strong over pretty</b>: steel crowns, amalgam, glass ionomer.</p>\n","cp":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>What CP is and what causes it</li><li>The types and their movements</li><li>Most have normal intelligence</li><li>Seizures, medicines, swallowing, reflux and drooling</li><li>Consent, the mouth, home care and wheelchairs</li></ul></div>\n<h4>What CP is</h4>\n<p>Cerebral palsy is brain damage early in life that affects <b>voluntary movement and posture</b>. It's <b>permanent</b> but <b>doesn't get worse</b>. It's the most common movement disorder in children (about 1 in 303), more likely in tiny or premature babies.</p>\n<h4>Causes</h4>\n<p>Most (70-80%) happen <b>before birth</b>: prematurity, infections (the <b>TORCH</b> group: toxoplasmosis, rubella, CMV, herpes), twins or triplets, bleeding in the brain. Some happen at birth (lack of oxygen, which turned out to be less common than thought). A few happen after birth: shaken baby, near-drowning, meningitis.</p>\n<h4>Types</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Spastic (70-80%)</b>: cortex damage; <b>stiff</b> muscles, strong reflexes, curled hands; loud noises can trigger spasms</li><li><b>Dyskinetic (10-20%)</b>: basal ganglia; <b>uncontrolled movements</b>: slow writhing (athetosis), shaking when trying to move (dystonic tremor) or fast jerks (chorea)</li><li><b>Ataxic (5-10%)</b>: cerebellum; poor <b>balance</b> and coordination</li></ul>\n<p>Limb words: hemiplegia = one side; diplegia = mostly legs; quadriplegia = all four.</p>\n<h4>Intelligence</h4>\n<p><b>Most people with CP are as smart as anyone.</b> Trouble speaking (dysarthria, up to 88%) doesn't mean trouble understanding. Always talk to them as a peer. Most can consent for themselves: by signing, saying yes with a witness, blinking, or a speech device. And they can say no.</p>\n<h4>Medical issues</h4>\n<p>About 30% have <b>epilepsy</b>. If a seizure starts: stop, take everything out of the mouth, turn them on their side, keep them safe, let it end (under 5 minutes), then send them home. A seizure over <b>5 minutes</b> (or back-to-back seizures) is <b>status epilepticus</b>: call EMS.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Dilantin</b> causes gum overgrowth; surgery waits until it's infected or covers the biting surfaces</li><li><b>Depakote</b> can lower platelets: bleeding</li><li><b>Bisphosphonates</b>: careful with extractions (bone necrosis)</li></ul>\n<p><b>Swallowing problems</b> mean a strong gag and choking risk: sit them up more and spray water in short bursts. Over half have <b>reflux</b>, which scoops out the enamel; fix the reflux before restoring. <b>Drooling</b> comes from poor swallowing, not too much saliva; drying the mouth can cause rampant decay, so treat it only as a last resort.</p>\n<h4>The mouth</h4>\n<p>Long, narrow faces, <b>open bites</b>, flared front teeth and <b>Class II</b> bites. Flared teeth plus falls and seizures mean many broken or knocked-out front teeth. A bit more decay, and up to 3 times more gum disease. Most grind their teeth (flat wear). Number of teeth, eruption timing and tongue size are normal.</p>\n<p>Patients fed by <b>g-tube</b> build heavy tartar: clean the cheek and tongue sides but leave it on the biting surfaces. Use steel crowns, glass ionomer and amalgam over fussy restorations.</p>\n<h4>Home care and wheelchairs</h4>\n<p>Make the toothbrush handle <b>fatter</b> (tennis ball, foam, washcloth) or <b>longer</b> (tongue blades). Waterpiks, disclosing tablets and rinses don't help much. Brush chlorhexidine on if they can't rinse.</p>\n<p>Let <b>caregivers</b> move a patient from the wheelchair, or treat them right in the chair: <b>lock the wheels</b> and tilt it back, often only to about 45 degrees.</p>\n","syndromes":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>The descriptive terms</li><li>Fragile X, Prader-Willi, tuberous sclerosis, Apert, Lesch-Nyhan, Pierre Robin, fetal alcohol syndrome, Cornelia de Lange</li></ul></div>\n<h4>Words for hands and eyes</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Brachydactyly</b> short fingers; <b>syndactyly</b> fused fingers; <b>clinodactyly</b> a bent finger; <b>ectrodactyly</b> missing fingers; <b>polydactyly</b> extra fingers</li><li><b>Coloboma</b> a hole in the iris; <b>miosis</b> small pupil, <b>mydriasis</b> big pupil; <b>strabismus</b> crossed eyes; <b>hypertelorism</b> wide-set eyes</li></ul>\n<h4>Tongue, spine, face</h4>\n<p><b>Glossoptosis</b>: the tongue sits low and back, which can cause a cleft palate and block the airway. <b>Scoliosis</b> curves the spine sideways (C or S); <b>kyphosis</b> hunches it forward (prop the head with pillows). <b>Meningomyelocele</b> pushes out spinal cord; <b>meningocele</b> only its covering. <b>Dolichocephaly</b> is a long, narrow face; <b>brachycephaly</b> a short, square one.</p>\n<h4>Fragile X</h4>\n<p>Too many repeats in the <b>FMR1</b> gene on the X chromosome. <b>Boys</b> are hit harder because they have one X. Late walking, hand flapping, poor eye contact (like autism), a <b>long face and big jaw</b>.</p>\n<h4>Prader-Willi</h4>\n<p>Missing genes on <b>chromosome 15</b> (the dad's copy). Endless <b>hunger and obesity</b>, mild to moderate ID, tantrums and stubbornness, and they may not feel pain. Thick, scant saliva means <b>heavy tartar</b>, cavities and gum disease. Obesity makes sedation risky.</p>\n<h4>Tuberous sclerosis</h4>\n<p>Benign tumors grow anywhere; the problems depend on where. Brain tumors mean seizures (and gum overgrowth from seizure drugs); in the mouth, weak enamel and gum growths.</p>\n<h4>Apert</h4>\n<p>Skull bones <b>fuse too early</b>. Long face, wide eyes, flat midface, <b>webbed hands</b>, airway problems. In the mouth: crowding, high palate, open bite, <b>Class III</b>, late teeth.</p>\n<h4>Lesch-Nyhan</h4>\n<p>Too much <b>uric acid</b> (HPRT1 gene), almost always boys. Severe <b>self-biting</b> of lips and fingers. Taking out all the teeth can be the kindest treatment.</p>\n<h4>Pierre Robin</h4>\n<p>A <b>tiny lower jaw</b>, a tongue that falls back, a U-shaped cleft palate. Usually normal intelligence. The danger is the <b>airway</b>: severe cases are treated asleep, intubated.</p>\n<h4>Fetal alcohol syndrome</h4>\n<p>From drinking during pregnancy. Small head, <b>smooth philtrum</b>, <b>thin upper lip</b>, small jaw. Mild to moderate problems with focus and judgment; many are never diagnosed.</p>\n<h4>Cornelia de Lange</h4>\n<p>Short, with <b>thick eyebrows</b>, moderate to severe ID and hard behavior. Small, <b>widely spaced</b> teeth that come in late; cleft palate in 20%.</p>\n","autism":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>What autism is and how common</li><li>Genes, twins and vaccines</li><li>Signs, stimming, meltdowns and sensory defensiveness</li><li>Dental problems in autism</li><li>Planning the visit and talking to the patient</li><li>Sedation, the OR and referring</li></ul></div>\n<h4>What autism is</h4>\n<p>Autism spectrum disorder is a developmental disability that causes <b>social, communication and behavior</b> challenges. It's now about <b>1 in 36</b> children (2020; 1 in 68 in 2012) and <b>four times more common in boys</b>. It's a spectrum: one teen can't speak, another is a math whiz who can't hold a conversation.</p>\n<p>The cause isn't known, but genes matter a lot: if one <b>identical twin</b> has autism, the other does <b>77%</b> of the time; for <b>fraternal twins</b> it's <b>31%</b>. About a quarter of cases trace to one powerful gene change, often brand new rather than inherited. <b>Vaccines don't cause autism</b>: that study was a fraud. Older fathers and valproic acid in pregnancy raise the risk.</p>\n<h4>Signs</h4>\n<p>It starts <b>before age 3</b>. A child may not answer to their name by 12 months, not point by 14 months, not pretend-play by 18 months; may avoid eye contact, repeat words (<b>echolalia</b>), get upset at small changes, flap hands or rock, and have no sense of danger. Babies who will develop autism look at mouths instead of eyes.</p>\n<h4>Stimming and meltdowns</h4>\n<p><b>Stimming</b> (flapping, rocking, repeating sounds) helps them cope. Ask about it ahead of time. If it isn't getting in the way, <b>let it be</b>; if it is, redirect their hands.</p>\n<p>A <b>meltdown is not a tantrum</b>. A tantrum is for attention and stops when the child gets what they want. A meltdown means the child can't cope: they don't care who's watching, may hurt themselves, and it fades slowly. Find out the triggers and warning signs beforehand, and be kind to the family.</p>\n<h4>Sensory defensiveness</h4>\n<p>Some brains feel ordinary sensations as painful, so the patient defends against touch to the face, bright lights, noises, paste texture, gloves and gagging. Pushed too far, they go into fight or flight. Help with: the <b>x-ray vest</b> (deep pressure), <b>sunglasses</b>, a warning before each step, headphones, <b>firm touch</b>, a fidget toy, something chewy beforehand.</p>\n<h4>Dental problems</h4>\n<p>No extra or missing teeth like Down syndrome or CP, but more <b>cavities</b> (picky diets, hard to clean), more <b>wear</b> (grinding), more <b>erosion</b> (reflux), slightly more gum disease, and more <b>injuries</b> (no sense of danger, more abuse).</p>\n<h4>Before the visit</h4>\n<p>Give the family a <b>practice kit</b> (prophy angle, mirror, bite holder without the metal bar) a few weeks ahead, and practice lying down with a dry toothbrush. Book the <b>first appointment of the morning</b>. Turn off music and sterilizers, dim the lights, offer sunglasses, have everything on the counter, skip perfume.</p>\n<h4>How to talk</h4>\n<ul><li>Short and literal: \"Open your mouth,\" not \"open like an alligator\"</li><li>\"<b>Hands on belly</b>\"</li><li>Say it once, then wait</li><li>Use the same words every time</li><li>A calm, lower, direct voice; praise calmly, no clapping and cheering</li><li>Don't ask yes-or-no when no isn't an option: \"It's time to polish your teeth.\"</li><li>Use pictures</li></ul>\n<h4>The visit</h4>\n<p>Follow a <b>picture schedule</b>. Start lying down, start at the <b>front teeth</b>, go from least to most invasive, and <b>count to 10</b> for breaks (always reach 10). Reward with praise or a video break.</p>\n<h4>When the office isn't enough</h4>\n<p>Oral sedation is mild and often not enough. IV sedation needs a special license and an IV the patient may not tolerate. General anesthesia works but adult OR access in Alabama is scarce. If you can't treat safely, you must <b>refer</b>: Sparks, UAB Hospital Dentistry, the Foundry, oral surgeons (extractions only), pediatric dentists.</p>\n","prevention":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Fluoride varnish and the ADA numbers</li><li>Fillings that prevent decay</li><li>Finding and removing tartar; prophy tips</li><li>Brushing aids and positions at home</li><li>Home fluorides</li><li>Drinks and diet</li></ul></div>\n<h4>In the office</h4>\n<p>Sparks uses only <b>fluoride varnish</b>: no trays to gag on, stronger, quick to paint on, and the patient can <b>eat and drink right away</b>. Foam and gel need four minutes in a tray.</p>\n<p>Varnish can stop small cavities from growing. <b>Sealant plus varnish</b> every 3-6 months stopped <b>67 of 100</b> early chewing-surface cavities; varnish alone stopped <b>46 of 100</b> early cavities between teeth. For root cavities, <b>Prevident 5000</b> at home.</p>\n<p>When you have to fill: <b>silver diamine fluoride</b> to stop small ones (it turns them black), <b>glass ionomer</b> (releases fluoride), or <b>steel crowns</b> for big ones.</p>\n<h4>Tartar and cleanings</h4>\n<p>Puffy gums with no visible tartar usually means a <b>ring of tartar under the gums</b>. Check! Use a <b>sickle</b> above the gums and a <b>curette</b> below. Tartar on the <b>front of the lower front teeth</b> means the lip blocks the brush: teach caregivers to pull the lip down.</p>\n<p>With the rubber cup, reach the gumline and roll between teeth. For gaggers, go in <b>1-2 second pulses</b>. <b>Tube-fed</b> patients build heavy tartar: clean the sides, but <b>leave it on the biting surfaces</b>. Many need cleanings every 3-4 months, even if insurance pays only twice a year.</p>\n<h4>At home</h4>\n<p>The <b>Surround</b> brush cleans three sides at once for patients who allow only a quick brushing. Fatten handles (tennis ball, foam, washcloth) or lengthen them (tongue blade). Electric brushes help many.</p>\n<p>Positions: standing in front is easiest and gags least; behind the seated patient controls the head; a <b>recliner</b> practices for the dental chair; in bed or on the floor (two people) gives the most control but the most gagging.</p>\n<h4>Home fluoride</h4>\n<p>No fluoride rinses or gels before <b>age 8</b> (they swallow it: fluorosis). Most can't rinse, so brush it on. <b>Prevident 5000</b>: brush it on at bedtime, wipe out the extra, don't rinse. <b>GelKam</b> is just fluoride. <b>MI Paste Plus</b> (not plain MI Paste) has fluoride. Rinses work only if they can swish, or with a Toothette for 30-60 seconds.</p>\n<h4>Diet</h4>\n<p>If brushing isn't possible, <b>sugar control</b> is the best tool. <b>Sweet drinks</b> are the worst: ask, \"What does he drink the most in a day?\" Best drinks: <b>water, plain milk, unsweet tea</b>. Juice is not on the list. Diet drinks and sweeteners are fine for teeth: <b>10 calories or less per serving</b> is safe. Keep them to 2-3 a day, and on special occasions, enjoy.</p>\n","mental":"\n<div class=\"tested\"><b>What's tested</b><ul><li>Mental illness vs intellectual disability</li><li>Depression, bipolar, schizophrenia, dementia, addiction: what to expect in the mouth</li><li>Drug side effects and interactions</li></ul></div>\n<h4>The reading</h4>\n<p>This unit is a reading (SAID Module 9), not a lecture. Dr. Mitchell wants you to focus on the <b>dental issues</b> of each illness.</p>\n<p><b>Mental illness</b> comes from a psychiatric or emotional disorder and can come and go. <b>Intellectual disability</b> is a permanent thinking deficit. About 1 in 6 people has a mental disorder; about 3% have ID.</p>\n<h4>Depression</h4>\n<p>People stop caring for themselves, so: <b>poor hygiene, rampant decay, gum disease, missing teeth, dry mouth</b> and facial pain. Antidepressants cause <b>dry mouth</b> (14% on Prozac, 45% on tricyclics), and patients sip sugary drinks to cope: offer saliva substitutes. With <b>tricyclics</b>, epinephrine is OK with care but levonordefrin and norepinephrine are not. With <b>MAO inhibitors</b>, no Demerol. Patients getting <b>ECT</b> may need a mouth guard, and loose teeth handled first.</p>\n<h4>Bipolar disorder</h4>\n<p>Swings between mania and depression. In <b>mania</b>, people brush too hard: scraped gums and worn necks of teeth. <b>Lithium</b> causes dry mouth and mouth sores, and <b>NSAIDs can raise lithium levels</b>.</p>\n<h4>Schizophrenia</h4>\n<p>Delusions, voices, flat emotions. Patients often neglect their teeth. Approach <b>slowly</b>, explain and show each step. Antipsychotics cause dry mouth and, long-term, <b>tardive dyskinesia</b>: constant lip, tongue and jaw movements. Be careful with sedatives (breathing).</p>\n<h4>Dementia</h4>\n<p>Memory loss that gets worse, including Alzheimer's. Expect falls, root decay, gum disease and dry mouth. <b>Do dental work early</b>, before cooperation is lost, and take full x-rays early. Older people keep drugs in the blood about twice as long: prescribe carefully. Talk simply, use names instead of \"he\" or \"it,\" and repeat.</p>\n<h4>Alcohol and drugs</h4>\n<p>Expect neglect, grinding, dry mouth, <b>oral cancer</b>, thrush, poor healing and <b>bleeding</b> (liver). Check bleeding tests before surgery. No epinephrine within <b>48 hours of cocaine</b>. For people in recovery, avoid habit-forming drugs (even nitrous); use NSAIDs and a long-acting numbing shot. People on <b>Antabuse</b> can't use mouthwash with alcohol.</p>\n"},"noteTables":{"intro":[{"title":"How the terms changed","type":"table","head":["Era","Term","Notes"],"rows":[["Early 1900s","Feeble-minded: moron (mental age 7-12), imbecile (2-7), idiot (under 2)","Medical terms at the time; culture turned them into insults"],["Mid 1900s to 2010","Mental retardation: mild, moderate, severe, profound, by IQ (under 70)","Became a playground insult"],["2010","Rosa's Law","Removed \"mental retardation\" from U.S. federal documents"],["2013","DSM-5","Made the same change: intellectual disability"],["Today","Intellectual disability (ID) or intellectual and developmental disability (IDD)","A diagnosis, not a description: person first"]]},{"title":"Where people with ID live and spend the day","type":"table","head":["Setting","Who","Examples from the lecture"],"rows":[["Home with family","Most people with ID","Many go to a day program during the day"],["Day programs","Adults who live at home or in a group home","United Ability (formerly United Cerebral Palsy), the Exceptional Foundation, the Arc of Central Alabama"],["Group homes","About 6-8 clients at a similar level, with caregivers","Arc PHP, the Learning Tree; they bring clients to appointments"],["Institutions","Only 1-2%, the most profoundly affected","Glenwood (Birmingham)"]]},{"title":"General vs severe disability","type":"table","head":["","Any disability","Severe disability"],"rows":[["U.S.","1 in 5 adults (over 53 million)","12.6% (about 39 million, 2015)"],["Alabama","1 in 3 adults: leads the nation","16.7% (about 800,000; 814,000 projected by 2030)"],["Most common type","Mobility (much of it the elderly)",""],["Sex","Females a little higher","Male and female the same (12.5 vs 12.7%)"],["Race","African Americans, then Hispanics, then whites","Whites and African Americans the same; Native Americans highest; Hispanics low"],["Highest state","","West Virginia 19.4%; Arkansas 17.1%, Kentucky 17%"]]}],"behavior":[{"title":"Active vs passive protective stabilization","type":"table","head":["","Active","Passive"],"rows":[["What holds the patient","A person","A device"],["Module 6 calls it","Physical restraint","Mechanical restraint"],["Examples","Head holds, hand guarding, therapeutic holds of arms and legs","Mouth props (Molt, Open-Wide), papoose board / Rainbow Wrap"],["Needs informed consent?","Yes","Yes"]]},{"title":"Express vs implied consent","type":"table","head":["","Express","Implied"],"rows":[["How it's given","In words or in writing, unambiguously","By the person's actions or conduct"],["Example","A signed, specific consent for a papoose board","An adult who comes in for an exam and cleaning"],["Covers","Treatment beyond routine care, and any stabilization","Routine care an average person expects"]]},{"title":"Mouth props","type":"table","head":["Prop","Where you'll see it","Notes"],"rows":[["Molt mouth prop","Pediatric and special needs dentistry","Opens like scissors and latches at any opening; hold it from outside; blades on the posterior teeth; no silicone covers (choking hazard)"],["McKesson bite block","The second-floor and other adult clinics","Rubber wedge; not used in special needs or pediatric dentistry; patients can crush it"],["Open-Wide mouth prop","At home (Specialized Care Co.)","Wood core in dense foam; about $20 for four on Amazon"]]}],"down":[{"title":"The three types of Down syndrome","type":"table","head":["Type","Share","What happens","Chromosomes"],"rows":[["Trisomy 21","94%","Nondisjunction in a gamete: an extra whole chromosome 21","47"],["Translocation","5%","Extra chromosome 21 material attached to another chromosome, usually 14","46"],["Mosaicism","1%","Nondisjunction early in the embryo, not in the gamete","Some cells 46, some 47"]]},{"title":"Risk by mother's age","type":"table","head":["Mother's age","Births with Down syndrome"],"rows":[["About 20","1 in 2,300"],["34-39","1 in 280"],["40-44","1 in 130"],["46 and over","1 in 65"],["Overall","About 1 in 700"]]},{"title":"Heart defects (about 50% have one)","type":"table","head":["Defect","Share of cardiac cases","Notes"],"rows":[["Atrioventricular canal defect (AV septal, endocardial cushion)","45-60%","Most common; atria and ventricles; repaired early"],["Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","32-35%","Repaired early; usually no antibiotics once healed"],["Atrial septal defect (ASD)","1-8%","Repaired early"],["Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","7%","The duct fails to close after birth"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","4-6%","VSD, pulmonary stenosis, thick right ventricle, aorta over the VSD; cyanosis; often needs prophylaxis"],["Mitral valve prolapse","14% by history, 57% by EKG","Patients often don't know"],["Aortic regurgitation","6% by history, 11% by EKG",""]]},{"title":"Oral findings in Down syndrome","type":"table","head":["Finding","Detail"],"rows":[["Malocclusion","Class III 30-70% (midface hypoplasia, not a big mandible); Class II 3-32%; posterior crossbite 71%; open bite 5%"],["Palate and spacing","Narrow palate; spaces between teeth"],["Tongue","Macroglossia; fissured tongue"],["Eruption","Delayed (primary and permanent); impacted canines and premolars; over-retained primary teeth"],["Tooth number and shape","Missing laterals 35-43% (vs 2%); peg or conical teeth; microdontia (short crowns); taurodontism"],["Periodontal disease","The most common dental condition: early onset in 60-95%, from age 6, faster"],["NUG","45% (vs 4% of others with IDD), without the usual fetid breath or pain"],["Caries","Usually lower (69-78% caries free in studies), but the evidence is mixed"]]}],"cp":[{"title":"Types of cerebral palsy","type":"table","head":["Type","Share","Brain area","Movement"],"rows":[["Spastic","70-80%","Cerebral cortex","Stiff muscles: hypertonicity, hyperreflexia, contractures; startled by loud noises"],["Dyskinetic","10-20%","Basal ganglia","Involuntary movements: athetosis (slow writhing), dystonic tremor, chorea (fast)"],["Ataxic","5-10%","Cerebellum","Poor coordination and balance, intention tremor"],["Mixed","","More than one area","A mix"]]},{"title":"Spastic CP: which limbs","type":"table","head":["Term","Limbs involved"],"rows":[["Hemiplegia","Arm and leg on the same side"],["Quadriplegia","All four, arms worse than in diplegia"],["Diplegia","Legs, with minimal arm involvement"],["Monoplegia","One limb (rare)"],["Paraplegia","Only the legs"],["Triplegia","Any three"]]},{"title":"A seizure in the chair","type":"table","head":["Step","Do"],"rows":[["Stop","Stop the procedure"],["Remove","Take everything out of the mouth"],["Side","Turn the patient on the side (aspiration)"],["Safe","Keep them from falling or hitting anything"],["Allow","Let it run its course: it should end in under 5 minutes"],["Release","Finish only urgent work, then send them home"]]},{"title":"Oral health in CP","type":"table","head":["Finding","Detail"],"rows":[["Face","Long and narrow (dolichofacial): weak muscles, hyperdivergent planes"],["Malocclusion","Open bite (kept open by tongue thrust), flared incisors, more overjet, Class II with a retrusive mandible"],["Trauma","Avulsions and incisor fractures: unprotected incisors plus falls and seizures"],["Caries","A little higher: hygiene, soft diet, hypoplasia, mouth breathing, anti-drooling drugs, food held in the mouth"],["Periodontal disease","Up to 3x more common; no more calculus, except g-tube patients"],["Bruxism","58%; flat wear; pulp exposures rare"],["Erosion and hypoplasia","25-35%; GERD and developmental"],["Normal","Number of teeth, eruption times, tongue size"]]}],"syndromes":[{"title":"Terms to know","type":"table","head":["Term","Means"],"rows":[["Brachydactyly","Short fingers"],["Syndactyly","Fused fingers or toes (the right number of bones, joined)"],["Clinodactyly","Permanent deflection (bend) of one or more fingers"],["Ectrodactyly","Congenital absence of one or more fingers or toes"],["Polydactyly","More than five digits on a hand or foot"],["Coloboma","A hole in a structure of the eye (iris, retina, choroid, optic disc); usually the iris"],["Miosis / mydriasis","Constricted pupil / dilated pupil"],["Strabismus","Misaligned eye: cross-eyed"],["Hypertelorism","Abnormal distance between paired organs; usually wide-set eyes"],["Glossoptosis","Tongue displaced downward and retracted; can cause cleft palate; Pierre Robin and Down syndrome"],["Scoliosis","Spine curved sideways (C-shaped or S-shaped)"],["Kyphosis","Spine curved with convexity backward (bent forward)"],["Meningomyelocele / meningocele","Herniation with spinal cord / meninges only"],["Dolichocephaly","Long, narrow face; hyperdivergent planes"],["Brachycephaly","Short, square face; strong, flat jaw line; parallel planes"]]},{"title":"The syndromes side by side","type":"table","head":["Syndrome","Cause","Intellect","Dental points"],"rows":[["Fragile X","FMR1 repeats on the X chromosome; mostly boys","Mental delay; autistic behaviors","Long face, prominent jaw"],["Prader-Willi","Chromosome 15q: paternal genes deleted (70%); usually new mutation","Mild to moderate","Obesity (sedation), outbursts, insensitive to pain, thick saliva: calculus, caries, gingivitis; high palate"],["Tuberous sclerosis","Single gene; benign tumors anywhere","Depends on where the tumors are","Seizure-drug gingival overgrowth, weak enamel, gingival fibromas"],["Apert","Craniosynostosis; almost always new; 1 in 65,000","","Midface deficiency, Class III, crowding, high palate, open bite, delayed eruption; syndactyly; airway"],["Lesch-Nyhan","HPRT1 mutation: too much uric acid; almost only males","Profound","Self-biting of lips and fingers: may need full-mouth extractions"],["Pierre Robin sequence","Micrognathia, glossoptosis, U-shaped cleft palate","Usually normal","Airway: severe cases treated in the OR intubated"],["Fetal alcohol syndrome","Alcohol in pregnancy (environmental)","Mild to moderate","Smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, small jaw, sometimes cleft"],["Cornelia de Lange","Slow growth before and after birth","Moderate to severe","Thick eyebrows, cleft 20%, delayed eruption, spaced teeth, microdontia; difficult behavior"]]}],"autism":[{"title":"Tantrum or meltdown?","type":"table","head":["Tantrum (typical child)","Meltdown (autism)"],"rows":[["Wants attention; checks whether anyone is reacting","Doesn't look or care whether anyone reacts"],["Avoids getting hurt","Doesn't consider their own safety"],["Tries to manipulate the situation","Struggling to cope with the environment"],["Stops as soon as the goal is reached","Runs on its own power and tapers off slowly; nothing satisfies until it's over"],["","No one feels in control"]]},{"title":"Dental conditions in autism","type":"table","head":["Condition","Incidence","Why"],"rows":[["Caries","Higher","Fixed diets (one patient drinks one brand of juice), cleaning not tolerated"],["Attrition","Higher","Grinding for anxiety relief or self-stimulation"],["Erosion","Higher","More GERD"],["Periodontitis","Slightly higher","Behavior; Medicaid's limited adult dental benefit"],["Trauma","Higher","Reduced sense of danger, habits, more abuse"],["Tooth number, malocclusion","No difference","Unlike Down syndrome or CP"]]},{"title":"Advanced care in Alabama","type":"table","head":["Where","What they offer"],"rows":[["UAB Hospital Dentistry (GPR)","Medically complex patients; some comprehensive adult care in UAB's ORs"],["Oral surgeons","OR or IV sedation, but extractions only"],["The Foundry Dental Clinic (GPR, Bessemer)","An anesthesiologist comes to the clinic; comprehensive adult care"],["Sparks Dental Clinic","Dental home for complex patients; Children's OR under 21; oral sedation and protective stabilization for older patients"],["Pediatric dentists","OR or in-office sedation for children; a few accept adults"],["General dentists (often AEGD or GPR trained)","Some accept special needs patients; one or two have hospital privileges"]]}],"prevention":[{"title":"ADA 2018 guideline: non-restorative care of carious lesions","type":"table","head":["Lesion","Best option","Result"],"rows":[["Non-cavitated occlusal","Sealant plus 5% fluoride varnish every 3-6 months","67 of 100 arrested (varnish alone: in the 40s)"],["Non-cavitated approximal","5% fluoride varnish every 3-6 months","2 times the chance of arrest or reversal: 46 of 100"],["Non-cavitated facial or lingual","Fluoride varnish (or gel)","Effective"],["Root caries","Prevident 5000 at home","Effective"]]},{"title":"Positions for brushing at home","type":"table","head":["Position","Good for","Gag risk"],"rows":[["Standing, facing the patient","Cooperative patients; little control of arms","Lowest"],["Patient sits, caregiver stands behind","Tall patients; controls the head","Some increase"],["Recliner, caregiver behind","Trains for the dental chair","More"],["Lying in bed","Patients who can't stand or prefer lying; more control","Most"],["Lying on the floor, two caregivers","Less cooperative patients: one holds head and hands, one brushes","Most"]]},{"title":"Home fluorides","type":"table","head":["Product","What it is","How to use"],"rows":[["Prevident 5000","High-fluoride toothpaste (sodium fluoride)","Brush on at night, spit or wipe out the excess, go to bed without rinsing"],["GelKam","Fluoride only (stannous fluoride), not a toothpaste","Same way, alongside a toothpaste they like"],["MI Paste Plus","Calcium and phosphate plus sodium fluoride","Same way (plain MI Paste has no fluoride)"],["Fluoride rinses (ACT, fluoride Listerine)","Mouthwash","Only if they can rinse; otherwise a Toothette for 30-60 seconds"]]}],"mental":[{"title":"Disorders and their dental implications","type":"table","head":["Disorder","Prevalence (reading)","Dental implications"],"rows":[["Major depression","About 6% severe; 3% hospitalized","Poor hygiene, rampant decay, advanced perio, missing teeth, bad prostheses, orofacial pain, xerostomia, poor diet"],["Bipolar disorder","About 1%; familial","Mania: abraded mucosa and cervical abrasion from over-brushing; depression: as above; lithium: stomatitis, xerostomia"],["Schizophrenia","1-2% hospitalized at some point","Neglect, xerostomia from antipsychotics, decay and perio; tardive dyskinesia"],["Dementia","2-3% at 65-79; over 20% at 80+","Falls (maxillofacial injury), ulcers, root caries, perio, broken teeth, xerostomia, candidiasis, useless prostheses"],["Alcohol and substance abuse","Alcoholism 28-50% in psychiatric hospitals","Neglect, bruxism, xerostomia, oral cancer, candidiasis, poor healing, trauma, bleeding"]]},{"title":"Drug cautions","type":"table","head":["Patient takes","Caution"],"rows":[["Tricyclic antidepressants","Epinephrine with prudence; levonordefrin (Neo-Cobefrin) and norepinephrine (Levophed) contraindicated; sedatives with care; xerostomia in 45%"],["MAO inhibitors","Epinephrine contraindicated (per the reading); meperidine (Demerol) absolutely contraindicated"],["SSRIs (Prozac)","Xerostomia in 14%"],["Lithium","NSAIDs can reduce its clearance (toxicity), mostly with long use; benzodiazepines with caution; xerostomia in 73%"],["Antipsychotics (phenothiazines)","Sedatives can deepen respiratory depression; epinephrine fine if normotensive"],["Cocaine within 48 hours","Local anesthetic with epinephrine strongly contraindicated"],["Disulfiram (Antabuse)","No alcohol-containing mouthwashes or products"],["Recovering addicts","Avoid mood-altering drugs, including nitrous oxide; prefer NSAIDs; a long-acting local (bupivacaine)"]]}]}});
